    "id": "1772757544469",
    "name": "Invita Contracting - FAQ.pdf",
    "type": "upload",
    "fileName": "Invita Contracting - FAQ.pdf",
    "filePath": "/Users/brettkitchen/AI VOICE IMPRINT/kb-widget/uploads/1772757544272-Invita Contracting - FAQ.pdf",
    "extractedText": "\n\nDemetra Harris - 785-600-2021 \nBrandt Betts - 785-506-8010, x1310 \nDocfly - to make PDF w/editable fields. Will allow 3 free downloads a month. \n \nCarrier Follow Up Tracking Sheet (shared w/magellan) \nhttps://magellanfinancial-my.sharepoint.com/:x:/r/personal/demetra_harris_magellanfinancial_co\nm/_layouts/15/Doc.aspx?sourcedoc=%7B65bca141-2a65-4799-adc7-651d587c18cd%7D&actio\nn=default&CID=008a8196-9c41-43ab-b0d1-a7ba940e9b8d&_SRM=2%3AS%3A65 \n \nProbably delaware life and global Atlantic for income and ibexis, american life for growth\n \n \nEQ and Delaware and Global will not let an individual pay a business account. \n \nIbexis and NA (our agents are annuity only unless agent asks for life) are JIT and will do \nbackgrounds when new business is submitted, but issue a temporary writing code.  \n \nCarriers that do Roth Conversion - Athene, NA, EQ, Allianz, AE, AL \n \nAML and Best Interest /Annuity Suitability Training Links by State: \nhttps://www.webce.com/catalog/courses/insurance-ce/arizona/life_and_health \n \nAllows resolicitation forms to back date product training: \n \n● Allianz \n● Global Atlantic – only allowed 1 strike, not multiple\n \n● North American \n  \nDoes NOT apply to state training, appointments or licenses.\n \n \nGHL \nMerging Contacts \n1. Select contacts > More \n2. With radio buttons, you can only have one selection, which check marks you can include \ninformation from both contacts. Anything selected will be merged and included on the \nnew contact card. \nCreate the Custom Field in GHL \n \nGo to Settings (in your sub‑account), Click Custom Fields, Click Add Field and choose the type \n(Text, Date, etc.), and select Bulk Actions to Edit or Delete. \n\n \nHow do we make sure we have all of our agents imported? Who is doing the double check - \nRussell Meinen should have been in the import, but I had to add him. \n \nHow do we get writing numbers over from our excel spreadsheet? Can it bring over comments?  \n \n \nHow will we apply the rainmaker and Contract active tag in GHL - when we are not using \nInfusion anymore? Will GHL still send out the Firelight, Core Carriers, email via automation \nwhen a contact is marked active?\n \nSteps to see if agent is NIGO \nVerify state agent is submitting the app \n● Verify all training is completed \n● Verify E&O is up to date and on file \n● Remind about product training\n \n \nChanging from Individual to Business Appointment \nHe'll send us the Agency License, EIN # for the DBA, a voided Business check \nUpdating Documents w/Carriers \n \nPlease see attached and update agents file: \n  \nName \nSSN (last 4 only) \nDOB  \n \nUpdating EFT w/the Carrier (each carrier must be processed \nindividually) \n1. From SureLC: Carrier Request > Payment \n2. At the end click Administrator > Process \n3. Forward the email to the carrier \n4. Upload to SureLC \n\n \nIf DD form is signed, all carrier will accept it. If the voided checks look a little less like a started \ncheck, I've cropped them then added them to the EFT sheet that they fill out and sign. So that is \nalso an option. \n \n● Requirements for EFT for ALL carriers is an Official Bank Letter that is Signed or \na Voided Check. \n● Agents can NOT have multiple EFTs set up for different carriers. It needs to be \nonly ONE EFT set up for all carriers as this causes extreme issues on the back \nend with commissions. \n● Soon we will be updating all Onboarding packets to reflect the issue with the \nBusiness/Individual EFTs for accounts as well. Once I have this in hand I will \nforward to you to update with your agents moving forward. \n \nCommissions reps are also able to walk you through EFT changes if you need help: \ncommissions@magellanfinancial.com\n \n \nUpdating E&O/AML w/the Carrier (can BC all carriers) \n1. Send email to my contracting email, cc Demetra, and BC all carriers. \n2. Upload new one to SureLC \n \nUpdating State Licenses \nYou do not need to upload it in SureLC. Once NIPR can see it’s active, our system will update. \nThis is not always the same day. Sometimes it can take a couple days, but we definitely do not \nlike to manually add state licenses. \n \nAgency license is about to expire. If you go to the DBA tab and scroll down to “Linked Firm \nInformation” and click “Firm Profile” you can view the agency licenses there. \nMagellan Training - SureLC \nI also wanted to mention a quick clarification regarding SureLC. Since it’s a national system, \nanytime an agent’s profile auto-populates, the data is pulling either directly from NIPR or from a \nprevious IMO, such as Advisors Excel. Because multiple organizations have access to those \nprofiles, the information is not always current. For that reason, when completing contracting, it’s \nimportant that we verify everything against the packet the agent actually signed—since their \nmost up-to-date information is often different from what SureLC pre-fills. \n \n\nInvita Contracting Process \n1. When you receive paperwork: \na. Save it in Box \nb. Enter ppw into SureLC \nc. Send it to Demetra \nd. Create Opportunity in GHL (put in last 4 of ss, DOB, and hierarchy) \n2. If everything is IGO: \na. Request carriers from SureLC (North American & Nationwide are not in our \nhierarchy) \ni. See document Demetra created. \nb. Give rainmaker tag in Infusion \n3. Send carrier confirmation email to agent and add contracts to GHL \n4. Follow up w/Carriers every 3 days using Carrier contact sheet \n(\nhttps://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1L0I47YfEUGMyJt2-CFlr8cVf9YzhHJ1s6yl-kRRM6ug/e\ndit?gid=0#gid=0\n) and update Demetra’s shared Invita Carrier Report spreadsheet \n(https://magellanfinancial-my.sharepoint.com/:x:/r/personal/demetra_harris_magellanfina\nncial_com/_layouts/15/Doc.aspx?sourcedoc=%7B5f4d8d2f-8ee0-464e-adae-0ea40acd6\nfc2%7D&action=default&CID=f7ee1839-d1b0-4965-92f8-563ee5b72859&_SRM=2%3A\nS%3A201) with your notes and GHL. Always write down who you spoke with. \n5. When you get approvals: \na. Send it to the agent using email template \nb. cc Demetra \nc. update GHL \n6. When first writing number comes through mark Contract Active in Infusion \n \nSureLC Tips \n● You do not want to check the box for activation email. \n● EFT is fine with no Signature if it’s an actual voided check \n● As long as the agent has completed & signed the EFT form in the packet and the voided \ncheck matches, no update needs to be made to SureLC. \n● Solicitors/DBA - not showing the company name \nI will do it but when you input a business who may have downlines you’ll check the box \n“Has Solicitors” on the DBA tab. This can be done at any time. I will review his \ninformation and let you know if it’s good to process. \n\n \n \nDarken a signature in Photoshop: \n1. Select the layer \n2. Select the rectangle (marquee tool) and drag it over the signature to select it \n3. Image > Adjustments > Levels \n4. Command D to deselect the Marquee tool and save as PDF \n5. Resave as JPG \n \nTracking Information \n \nInfusion \nhttps://ironfist.infusionsoft.com  \n \nUsername: rich@producermastery.com \nPassword: \nOc3@nR3d^\n \n \nLook up agent information, like phone \nnumber and program information \nRing Central \njona@brettandethan.com / Ring2025! \n \n \nSureLC \nSuranceBay Support Desk \nsupport@surancebay.com \n\nLicensing2026! \nhttps://www.surelc.com/index.html \n \nhttps://surelc.surancebay.com/bga/producers\n?search=&branch=Invita%20Financial&looku\npType=all&preset=Original%2FFlat&autoSav\ne=false&advanceMode=false \n \nhttps://support.surancebay.com/hc/en-us/artic\nles/360060348713-SureLC-Logins-Password\ns#h_01ERNDZ848NWE632Q5CYQEG75B \nGo High Level \nProducer$2125 / \ncontracting@invitafinancial.com \n \nhttps://app.producerautopilot.com/agency_da\nshboard/\n \n \n \nInvita Contracting Email \ncontracting@invitafinancial.com \n \nPassword: Production10! \nC0ntr@ct!ng@IF \n \n \nInvita Tracking Sheet \n \nhttps://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1rqf3\nTI7w-k12xxoWcijBgPiBvzrTp2g3DaBqn9vW\nQV0/edit#gid=581428272 \n \n \nCompleted Agent Paperwork \n \nhttps://worthlandcapital.app.box.com/folder/2\n48426567778 \n \nUsername: rbradley@producerelite.com \nPassword: Purpl3Pr0duct10n \n \nTest Secure Portal: \nhttps://app.box.com/folder/268613636404?s=\ns9vatmlpnqbs5sdya80dt1f28vcf3u5x \n \nUsername: contracting@invitafinancial.com \nPassword: T1$@F3o!*3N^\n \nSave Magellan paperwork in Box under agent \nname \n \n \n \n \n \nIf using the test portal, create a folder titled \nwith the agent’s name. Add permissions - this \nshould only be view and upload. And then \nsend the agent the link to upload documents \nto the secure portal. They must let you know \nwhen they’ve uploaded the documents. \n\nContacting A Carrier on Behalf of \nAn Agent \n \nhttps://worthlandcapital.app.box.com/file/645\n781198227\n \n \nCarrier Contact Sheet - Contracting \nhttps://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1L0I47Yf\nEUGMyJt2-CFlr8cVf9YzhHJ1s6yl-kRRM6ug/edit?\ngid=0#gid=0 \nYou will need the information on this google \nsheet along with the agent’s social security \nnumber. The SS number can be found on his \ncontracting paperwork, accessed via Box. \nBrett and Ethan Members Area \n \nhttps://brettandethan.com/members/ \n \ncontracting@brettandethan.com \njona2025\n \n \n \nMagellan Contacts \n-   Magellan Firelight: Brandt Betts; bbetts@magellanfinancial.com \n-  Email contracting@magellanfinancial.com for any contracting questions. Jamie Votaw \nis the director. Only email her with specific questions that contracting can’t answer.  \n- Send illustration requests to: illustrations@invitafinancial.com \n- For questions about new business: Alicia Padilla; Magellan new business \n- Kamry in contracting - 785-506-8041; Demetra Harris - 785-600-2021 \nCarriers \n- Agents do not have access to Annexxus products through Magellan. Annexxus \nproducts are considered a completely new line of business, so an agent can have both \nannuity and annexxus products at two different FMOs. \n \n- If you are contacting a carrier about an agent, you will need the information on this \nspreadsheet, as well as the agent’s social security number or writing number: \nhttps://worthlandcapital.app.box.com/file/645781198227. Ask for the licensing and \ncommissions department. \n \n- Carriers can give you information about completed or expired training. You can also \nview this information on the carrier website. \n\n \n-  Equitrust requires agents to complete product training before they will appoint them. \n \n \n \n \nAthene* (A+) \nhttps://www.athene.com/ \n888-266-8489 (option 1, then 2, then 3 - \nagency services) \n \nUsername: Imav3ricks \nPassword: \nT1$7^0NOx!ZBa2a\n \n \nBrandon Keeley \n763-582-6406 \n \nAnnuities ONLY \n \n \n- Login as a financial professional \n- My Business > Downline Search > \nsearch by agent name \n- Does not like voided check to be \nin agency name if agency is not \nlicensed. \nAllianz* (A+) \nhttps://www.allianzlife.com/\n \n \nUsername: \nNorthNest360\n \nPassword: \nS28*1PIzca \n \n800.950.7372 \n \nBrandon Keeley \n763-582-6406\n \n \nLife and Annuities \n \n- Knowledge Center > Training \nTracker > Look up (search by last \nname) \n- Life numbers start with 358; \nannuity numbers start with 722 \n- Accounts > Agent Recruiting > \nSSN - to view what level an agent \nis at. \n \nTo contract with Allianz Preferred, agents \nmust meet one of the following and \nsubmit proof with contracting paperwork. \nIf an agent has an agency, they must \nwrite a LOE stating they are the only \nproducing agent for that business. If there \nare downline agents, they can qualify off \nof premium written, via a TAP report in \njust this instance with $500,000 of annuity \npremium written. But the principal agent \nmust always be contracted first and also \nshow proof of qualifying before downline \nagents can be considered. \n \n\n \nAmerican Equity (A) \nhttps://www.american-equity.com/ \n888-221-1234 \n0156517 \n \nPW \nT1$7^0NOx!ZB\n \n \n \nAnnuities ONLY \n \nMailing FBO check: Send overnight mail \nw/tracking number to the following \naddress: \n \n6000 Westown Parkway \nWest Des Moines, IA 50321 \n \nAmerican Life  \n(not available in CA or PA) \nhttps://www.american-life.com/ \nPhone: 402-489-8266 \n \nrbradley@invitafinancial.com \nT1S7^0NOx!ZB \nAnnuities ONLY \n \n \n \n \nAmerican Life’s release process is quite \ndifferent from most other carriers. They \nrequire a signed release for any active \nagent—regardless of whether the agent \nhas submitted business or not. \n  \nHowever, there is a workaround. If the \nagent (or new FMO) can provide proof \nthat a release request was emailed to the \ncurrent FMO, and there is no response \nwithin 10 business days, American Life \nwill proceed with the transfer. At that \npoint, they will notify the current FMO of \nthe pending transfer and approve it. \n \n\nDelaware Life (A-) \nPreappointment states: OR, PA and TX \nJust in time for all others \n● Runs backgrounds when business \nis submitted \n \nhttps://www.delawarelife.com/product/growt\nh-pathway \n800-374-3714 \n \nLicensing.support@delawarelife.com \n \nhttps://portal.delawarelife.io/V2/auth/dashbo\nard \n \nrbradley@invitafinancial.com \nT1$7^0NOx!ZB \n \nAnnuities ONLY \nEquitrust \nhttps://agents.equitrust.com/ \n866-598-3692 (agents and licensing) \n \nUsername: 63012 \nPassword: \nS2$6^1BPv#XN2a\n \n \nDustin Carlson \nDustin.Carlson@equitrust.com \n \nWriting number doesn’t change when \nmoving between FMOs - tied to agent’s \nSS. \n \nAnyone active with EquiTrust has access \nto the Bridge product.  The agent (and \nagency if there is one) must hold the \nHealth line of authority in the state of \nsolicitation and the agent must be LTC \ntrained in the state as well.  Along with the \nEquiTrust PST that is required to appoint. \nAnnuities and Life \n  \n- Agent Administration > Active \nAgents > Export report \n \nEquitrust Product Training must be \ncompleted within 30 days from the \nappointment request or the request will be \nclosed.  We may re-open if the Product \nTraining is completed within 90 days from \nthe date the contracting application was \nreceived. \n \nThe only time new business is required is \nif the agent was previously active and \nterminated for lack of production. \n \nAnnuity product training can be completed \non the EquiTrust agent website by using \nthe following link: EquiTrust Annuity \nProduct Training \n \nThe agent can submit a Letter of \nInstruction requesting to be paid as \nindividual for all South Carolina business \nand state that the agent is aware he will \nreceive a 1099 on his Social Security \nNumber. Going this route, they would \nneed to submit a signed agent contract \nand advise what EFT to use. If they want \nto use the one that is on file for the \n\nagency, that is all they would need to say \nin the letter. \nThe agent can write the business, but we \nwill not release commissions under the \nagency until the agency is licensed. \n \nWe have 45 days to backdate \nappointments in the state of South \nCarolina.  So either way, we should be \nokay. \n \nEmail deliver receipts to \ncustomer.service@equitrust.com \n \n● Within 0-6 months: the state \nappointment and the \nnew-business application will be \nprocessed \n● Within 7-24 months: the agent \nmust submit a signed Agent \nVerification Form to affirm the \ninformation on the original \nappointment application is still \ncorrect \n● After 24 months: the agent must \nsubmit a Background \nAuthorization form for re-review \nof agent’s background. \n \nBridge - hybrid product (life product that is \nannuity funded - they need annuity, BI, \nLTC training. \nAnytime you write LTC, you have to have \nthe health line of authority on your \nlicense. \n \nCertainty select (under the heading of \nnon-indexed annuity) \nF&G (A) \nhttps://saleslink.fglife.com/ \n \nUsername: \nrbradley@invitafinancial.com \nPassword: T1$7*0NOx!ZBa \n \nYou can send licenses to F&G directly at: \ncontracting.support@fglife.com \n \nJust include the agent's name & social or \nLife and Annuities \n \n- Agent > hierarchy report > search \nby last name. \n- Download report for all agents \n- R7 prefix indicates annuity # \n- The only time I have seen any \nadditional training for life, is if the \nlife product has long term care \nwith the product, which then the \nagent will need to complete CE for \n\nNPN for verification purposes. \n \n800.445.6758, option 4 \n \nlong term care... otherwise you \nshould be good. \n \nGlobal Atlantic (A) \nhttps://professionals.globalatlantic.com/\n?check_logged_in=1 \n \nInvitaFinancial \nS2025^!9$$a \n \nAnnuity ONLY \n \nForecare - hybrid product (life product that \nis annuity funded - they need annuity, BI, \nLTC training. \nAnytime you write LTC, you have to have \nthe health line of authority on your \nlicense. \nIbexis.com (A-) \n(866) 376-1669 \n \nPreappointment in PA, CO, UT, WY, WA \nAll other states JIT \n \nhttps://ibexis.com/wealthdefender \nrbradley@invitafinancial.com \nS28*1PIzca \n \nMailing Address \nPO Box 84008 \nLincoln, NE 68501 \n \nOvernight Mailing Address \n777 Research Dr. \nLincoln, NE 68521 \n \nAgent Licensing and Commission \nibexisagentcontracting@nttdata.com \n \nAgent New Business \nibexisnewbusiness@nttdata.com\n \nAnnuity ONLY \nNorth American* (A+) \n*Not in our hierarchy \n \nPA requires preappointment \nLife and Annuities \n \n(They are separate contracts; we appoint \nyour agents for annuity and then add life if \nrequested when new business is present.) \n \n*appointment closes if they don’t write \nbusiness in 6 months \nProsperity Life (A-) \n \n \nAnnuity ONLY \n\nAIG Corebridge (American \nGeneral) \n \nhttps://connext.corebridgefinancial.com/l\nife/connext-security/public/login\n \n \nrbradley@invitafinancial.com \nT1$7^0NOx!ZB \n800-247-8837 \n800-255-2702, option 3 - contracting \n \nYou can view the agents status on the \nAIG/Corebridge portal once he is made \nactive by searching his name in the \nContracting tab. \n \nMatt Bernstein \nCell 818.451.0203 | IMO Sales Desk \n888.438.6933 opt. 2 \nMatt.Bernstein@corebridgefinancial.com \n \nNicole Percey \n615-336-0080 \nnicole.percey@corebridgefinancial.com \n \nLife and Annuities (but stay away from \nannuities with AIG) \n \nThe GIWL is the Guaranteed \nIssued Whole Life line of business \nof Corebridge. This line is NOT \njust in time. \nTalcott \nAnnuity ONLY \n \nHello! \n  \nA link has been sent for you to complete \nsome pre-requisites for the appointment \nand confirm everything.  This carrier does \nrequire some extra steps/training for the \nappointment.   Product training is included \nin the steps you will take through the link \nsent your way. \n  \nThanks! \n \nAspida \nhttps://distribution.aspida.com/ \n1 (833) 427-7432 \n \nUsername: e@brettandethan.com \nPassword: Fabfour1* \n \n \n \n \n- Select all products, and select \n“street” level \n \nIf the agent is already active with Aspida, \nthe attached is all you would need to \ncomplete. If they told you a release is \nrequired, the attached plus a release is \nrequired. These forms are available to \nyou on the Aspida website under Forms \n\n \non the right hand side of the main page. \n \n \n \nCorebridge \nDual is allowed for annuity & life, but not the specialty lines like GIWL or AGLA. \nNew business is required to establish a new request under a new FMO.  That \nrequest goes active once that agent submits the case through that new FMO. \n \nTransfers: Corebridge does not transfer agent appointments but they do allow an agent \nto have many different appointments under various FMO’s. Agent needs to submit client \nname and product name to get contracting process started. He will still have access to \nhis current code/book of business and then work to establish another one under \nInvita/Magellan. \n \nQuest CE is no longer approved for AML for this carrier. \n \nWith Corebridge there is no transfer of appointments.  They would establish a new \nwriting number/relationship for the agent under Invita/Magellan with the submission of \nbusiness through our office.  Anything he’s previously done will remain under the FMO it \nwas written with.\n \nMailing Checks \nFYI -  Overnight mail to Corebridge for checks:  \n \nJPM  Chase - AGL 100330 \n2710 Media Center Dr. \nBldg #6 Suite 120 \nLos Angeles, CA 90065-1750  \nFor regular mailing checks \n \nAmerican General Life \nPO Box 100330 \nPasadena, CA 91189-0330 \nDigital Room \nhttps://eng2e.seismic.com/ls/b2b08e9a-ec16-4db3-82db-84bfd938e07d/f6J9_lY_bbOqXoYn#/ \n\n \nContacts \nCheck to see if agent is in good order to write business: \nannuitycentral@corebridgefinancial.com  \n \nYou can view the agents status on the AIG/Corebridge portal once he is made active by searching \nhis name in the Contracting tab: \nhttps://www.connext.corebridgefinancial.com/life/connext-producer-dashboard/public/login\n \n \n \nMatt Bernstein \nCell 818.451.0203 | IMO Sales Desk 888.438.6933 opt. 2 \nMatt.Bernstein@corebridgefinancial.com \n \nNicole Percey \n615-336-0080 \nnicole.percey@corebridgefinancial.com\n \nJust in Time Carriers  \nCarriers That Need A Client Name And Product: \n MassMutual \n Liberty Bankers  \n North American - issues a pending code (expires after 6 months, to get recontracted, \nsubmit client name and proof of product training). North American is considered a just in \ntime carrier for all states except PA. If the agent is NOT active with another FMO and the \nproduct training has been completed, business may be submitted using our platform, \nmark “pending” in place of a writing number. \n Nassau \n Securian \n TransAmerica \n Symetra \n One America \n GILICO \n Prudential Life (not just in time if writing in the state you live in) \n Lincoln Financial Group \n \nThese carriers typically assign a pending agent code, and the agent is in their system for a \ncertain number of months (usually 6)  in anticipation of new business. The above carriers \nrequire a client name to process.  \n \n\nOnce new business is received, the contracting process is finally completed, including \napplication review and background check. For some carriers (like North American), \nif no new \nbusiness is written within 6 months, the code will expire. \n \n \nCarriers That Need A Product Name and Training, Client Name, And State: \n AIG/Corebridge (Pennsylvania, you can appoint right away) \n Prudential (is NOT just-in-time if writing in the state you live in. clarify product: Life or \nAnnuity) \n Nationwide Life (clarify product: Life or PEAK 10) \n Securian (also known as Minnesota Life) \n Lincoln Life (also need DOB) \n \nNOTE:  \n \nIdeally, Magellan wants the above information submitted, along with the appointment \nrequest, as it gives us a head start on the contracting side.This will eliminate the \nchances of any potential NIGO’s and allows us to confir. all necessary training \ncompliance prior to the agent signing the application. \n \nNationwide - this carrier uses an agent's SSN as their writing number.  \nAIG/Corebridge - you can mark “pending” or use your NPN or social security \nnumber when submitting the first piece of business. Agents will gain full access \nto AIG/Corebridge after the first case issues. \n \n- No product training is required for life products at AIG/Corebridge unless written \nin Iowa. New York products for life are listed as US Life, not Corebridge. There \ncould be multiple requirements, please provide agent name to Magellan. \nBusiness can be written through Firelight (annuities) or iPipeline (life). you can \nfind information for this on our website in our VIP portal which requires no login to \nview resources. www.magellanfinancial.com \n \n- The GIWL is the Guaranteed Issued Whole Life line of business of Corebridge. \nAIG GIWL requires agents to be fully appointed for this line of business and is \nnot just in time. \n \n- PA requires pre-appointment, and this takes 3-5 business days. \n \n- Corebridge DOES assign a different writing number for each product series. \n \n\n- We can access agent information, including illustrations, new business app kits \nand product brochures on the AIG/Corebridge Connext website. It’s the same site \nwe log into to view our downline, new business & commissions. Once you’re \nlogged in, the middle of your home page will offer you most of this information \nwithout having to fish for it. Team Hurtig is available as well. \n  \nhttps://www.connext.corebridgefinancial.com/life/connext-portal/app/home \nContracting Process \nFor an agent to write life or annuity business, they first need to be appointed with an insurance \ncarrier(s). This process is called contracting. \n  \nAgent appointments and contracting can take 7 – 14 business days. If the agent is currently \ncontracted through another FMO, this is referred to as a transfer and this can be completed \nwithin a couple of days after submitting the contracting paperwork. \nCore carriers \n● Allianz (life) \n● Allianz Preferred (annuities) \n● American Equity (annuities ONLY) \n● American Life (not in CA) \n● Athene (annuities ONLY) (doesn’t do background until you submit business) \n● AIG/CoreBridge (JIT; life and annuities) \n● Delaware Life \n● Equitrust (annuities ONLY) \n● Fidelity and Guaranty Life (F&G) (life and annuities) \n● Global Atlantic (does not allow an IND to pay commissions to a business account) \n● Ibexis (JIT) \n● North American (JIT/6 month temp code; life and annuities) \n● Prosperity Life \n \nDual Appointment \n● Nationwide – dual appointment is allowed for life, but not annuities. \n● Allianz Life, Athene, Sentinel Security Life, SILAC, Nassau, North American, do NOT \nallow dual contracting. \n● AIG/Corebridge, Lincoln, Protective, and Symetra offer annuities and allow an agent to \ndual contract \n● F&G confirms no dual contracting is allowed. If an agent is not eligible to transfer they \ncan submit a letter of intent to transfer and continue doing business under the current \nFMO until the time line expires. Once the time hits, the letter of intent along with the new \n\ncontracting request from the new FMO serves as enough to transfer the agent at that \ntime. \n● Corebridge allows for dual contracting, so it is possible for agents to write Corebridge \nannuity business through multiple IMOs/FMOs and would have writing codes for each \nfirm and each product series. \n● Only Corebridge term life and annuity offers this. However, we do have more- \n \n○ Lincoln \n○ Symetra \n○ Banner \n○ Principal \n○ Protective \n \nContracting Steps \nIf an agent wants to get contracted: \n \n1. Send an email with the following: \n \n- Magellan contracting paperwork \n- Invita Contact Sheet \n- Allianz Preferred Qualifications document \n \nNOTE: If an agent has their comp paid to the agency, they must also provide a letter \nstating the below. “I am the sole owner and sole writing agent for the commissions listed \nin the 1099’s provided for my Allianz Preferred qualifications.” Downline agents never \ncount and must qualify on their own merit. If you submit a direct deposit form with Athene \nor Global, they will mail a check unless you submit a voided check or bank letter. \n \nNOTE: $50,000 in commissions for Allianz Preferred must be verified by a 1099 from the \nprevious year (unless we are in the 4th quarter, then they accept current year production \nas shown on a carrier commissions statement), payable to the producer directly from a \ncarrier. A 1099 can be in an agency name as long as the agent sends in the proper letter \nstating he/she is the sole owner and sole writing agent for the agency and the 1099 \nprovided. Current year-to-date production is not considered until 4th quarter. If an agent \nworks for a company, he can submit a TAP report in PDF format that shows at least \n$500,000 in annuity premium written. \n \nNOTE: The following items are required to change an individual appointment to a \nbusiness appointment for commissions & tax purposes: \n \n● EIN issued by IRS \n● Insurance license for the agency in all states agent is licensed. \n\n● E&O listing agency name (alternatively, they can reach out to the insurance \nprovider and ask them to add agency name as a DBA on the certificate this will \ntake care of having the agency listed but without the liability).\n \n● Voided check – if licensed agency name is different from the one already listed \non the check we have \n● Articles of Incorporation/meeting minutes/docs filed with the state department of \nfinance and insurance showing agent is the owner \n \nSample Email Copy: \n \nAttached is the Magellan paperwork for you to complete to start the contracting process. \n \nPlease note that Allianz Preferred (annuities) has agent requirements (see attached \ndocument for more information). If you are interested in being contracted with this carrier, \nplease include proof of how you qualify when you return your completed paperwork.\n \n \n \n \n2. When the paperwork is returned, doublecheck the following: \n \n1) Completed Questionnaire (include an explanation if agent checked “No” to any \nquestion) \n2) Signed Signature Page \n3) Signed Disclosure Release Page \n4) Completed EFT Authorization Page (attach a copy of a voided check to this page) \n \nNOTE: If the agent does not have an agency check, the carriers accept a bank \nletter drafted by the bank and signed by a bank official, indicating the account \nname, account number, and bank routing number. \n \n5) A copy of your individual and/or corporation state insurance license(s) \n6) A copy of your E&O coverage (coverage should be at minimum 1,000,000) \n \n\n3. Send the completed paperwork to contracting@magellanfinancial.com and cc \nJVotaw@magellanfinancial.com \n  \nMagellan “scrubs” the paperwork and inputs the agent’s information in SureLC. Magellan \nwill notify Invita if there are any outstanding requirements; you will need to contact the \nagent for any missing information.  \n \nNOTE: The carrier will not tell us why an agent was declined– it’s confidential and the \nagent must call the carrier directly. We also can’t help an agent resolve any issue with \nbackground checks. These are confidential and must be managed by the agent \nthemselves directly with the carrier. \n \n4. Next, send the agent an email with Magellan Firelight Instructions and attach the Product \nTraining Guide (see Magellan Firelight in this document for more information). \n \n5. Save the agent’s paperwork in box in a folder with their name: \nhttps://worthlandcapital.app.box.com/folder/248426567778 \n \n(FIG agent contracting data is found here): \nhttps://worthlandcapital.app.box.com/folder/248426567778 \n \n6. Apply RM (Rain Maker) tag to the agent in Infusion. In Infusion, search for the agent by \nname. Click on the agent name and scroll down and click on the button that says \nManage Tags. Scroll down to the AR dropdown box and select Rainmaker Webinar > \nApply Tag.\n \n \n7. Move the agent to Phase 2 of the Contracting Tracking Sheet, and update agent \ninformation. \n \n8. Communicate contracting status updates and writing numbers to the agent during the 2 \nweek processing window. \n \nOnce the insurance company completes its review and background checks, the \ninsurance company will issue the agent a writing code. This is the code the agent will \nuse on the application when they write business with that specific insurance company.  \n \nEach insurance company will have a unique writing code for the FMO/IMO and the agent \nassociated with that FMO/IMO. The assigned code is how the insurance companies \norganize the different FMO/IMO’s and their agents to know who to pay when cases are \ncomplete. \n \n9. When writing numbers start to come in, include the agent on the Writing Numbers tab of \nthe Contracting Tracking Sheet, and consult Box to see what carriers the agent originally \nselected. Put an “x” underneath any carrier that the agent requested so we can easily \n\ntrack processing. When you receive writing numbers, replace the “x” with the writing \nnumber so we know it’s been processed. \n \n10. Keep Account Managers in the loop when corresponding with agents: \n(https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1z32hqbZwW_QUZTsvjnu_ClJkcOhJxw479Tz\nYBGr2jt0/edit#gid=2099468459) \n \nAdding A Carrier \nIf an agent would like to contract with an additional carrier after the contracting process is \ncomplete, simply inform Magellan’s contracting department \n(contracting@magellanfinancial.com), by email, of the additional carriers the agent wants to \ninclude. \n \nLSW is a carrier we offer, LSW is the annuity and NLG is the life. \nThe GIWL is the Guaranteed Issued Whole Life line of business of Corebridge. AIG GIWL \nrequires agents to be fully appointed for this line of business and is not just in time. \nProduct Training Required Before Appointment \n● Equitrust \n● Ibexis \nAgent Training \nTo be compliant to sell annuities in states other than the agent’s home state, the agent will need to \nhave a non-resident license in that state. If he has questions about this, he would need to check with \nthat state's DOI (Department of Insurance). \n \nProduct Training \nBefore an agent can submit business, they must first complete product training for the specific \nproduct they are selling. The agent can use the Product Training Guide for instructions on how \nto complete the training for each carrier. \n \n- American Equity does not generate a certificate. \n- Equitrust requires agents to complete training before they will appoint them. \n  \n- The agent does NOT need a writing number to complete the product training on RegED \n(follow the non-registered agent instructions). They just need the product code. \n \n\nResolicitation Form: If the agent completed the product training AFTER the day he \nsubmits the app, he can fill out a resolicitation form with Allianz, Global Atlantic, North \nAmerican rather than resubmitting the app. Athene does not offer any exceptions. \n- For Corebridge product training, Manage Appointments tab – needs to select \n“Corebridge Financial”, for Broker Dealer tab – needs to select “Independent \n(Unaffiliated)” This should be the very first option listed in the BD dropdown menu. If they \ndon’t see the training listed, they could have already selected it and then it would be \nunder the Home tab. \n- No product training is needed for life products until they are writing LTC. \nAnnuity Training \nMost states will reciprocate on Best Interest training, but others, like CA and TX, have their own \ntraining and do not reciprocate.\n \nBest Interest Training \nNearly every state requires producers that work with annuity products to complete \nstate-specified levels of annuity suitability training, called Best Interest Training. States \nrequire Best Interest training for annuity sellers to protect consumers from predatory \npractices and ensure that consumers are treated fairly.  \n \nFor CA: \nhttps://www.webce.com/catalog/courses/course-information/insurance-ce/course/253084 \n  \nhttps://www.webce.com/catalog/courses/course-information/insurance-ce/course/251971 \n \n \nThis Best Interest training is a 4-hour Annuity Suitability course which can be completed \non WebCE or RegED. \n \nIn general, RegEd offers course 490 to fulfill the 4-hour course requirement. If the \nproducer is eligible to take a 1-hour course instead, course 491 is recommended. The \nprerequisite in taking the 1-hour option is that the producer has already completed \ncourse 390 or an equivalent course.  \n \nRefer to this chart: \nhttps://secure.reged.com/common/memberpages/REGEDVU/ANNforCEA_CECMaster.p\ndf \n \nOr this site is helpful: \nhttps://www.adbanker.com/licensing-requirements/insurance/iowa/continuing-education/ \n \n\nNOTE: We do not recommend LIMRA for BI training because they don’t generate a \ncertificate. If training is done via LIMRA, the agent will need to provide Magellan \nwith his LIMRA password so Magellan can generate a proper certificate.\n \n \nQ: Is Best Interest Training Required for Non-Resident States? \nThe Best Interest training is only required for the agent’s resident state. Most \nstates reciprocate with training, except for CA and TX. These two states have \ndifferent training requirements. California requires the 8-hour training, and Texas \nhas its own 4-hour training.\n \n \nIf the agent is compliant in their resident state that doesn’t require the BI training \nyet, but holds other non-resident licenses in states where it’s required, Magellan \nwill let you know the agent would be non-compliant in those states.  \n \nQ: What if an agent is new or has questions about Best Interest training? \nIf an agent is new, or has questions about this training, the information on this \nFAQ from RegED could be helpful: \nhttps://www.reged.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/01/AnnuityTraining-BestInterest\nStandardFAQs.pdf \n \nThis site lists each state and their requirements: \nhttps://www.adbanker.com/licensing-requirements/insurance/iowa/continuing-edu\ncation/ \n \nFor California Only \n \nEight hours of training is initially required, plus a four-hour CE training course every two \nyears, thereafter. A listing of the courses approved by the Department can be accessed \non this Web site using the following link: education provider/course search. Be sure to \nselect \"Life\" as the License Type, \"Continuing Education\" as the Education Type, and the \n\"Annuity 8-Hour\" as the Category Type. \nAML Training (Anti Money Laundering) \nFederal law requires all agents to complete anti-money laundering (AML) training. Some \ncompanies, like American Equity and American General, also have additional \nrequirements for their employees. They can take it at LIMRA: \nhttps://www.limra.com/en/solutions-and-services/onboarding-and-development/complian\nce-education-platform/u.s2.-anti-money-laundering-aml-training/ \n \nWebsite to reset and follow the prompts to reset password: \nhttps://knowledge.limra.com/ResetPassword.aspx \n \nYour LIMRA username is your NPN number. If you are a first-time user your password is \nyour last name all in lowercase. Please call LIMRA at 866-364-2380 for assistance using \ntheir website. \n\n \nIf you provide us with your LIMRA login information, we can sync it to SureLC to \nretrieve your AML, from then it will automatically update whenever you take a new \ncourse. \n \nList of Approved Vendors: \nLIMRA \nSuccessCE \nSandi Kruise, Inc. \nQuestCE \nA.D. Banker \nRedED \nWebCE \nKaplan Financial \nExamFX \nBetter CE \n \nFor Corebridge Only - AML course must be completed within the last two years through \nan approved vendor (list below): \nLIMRA \nRegEd \nSuccessCE \nQuestCE \nWebCE \nKaplan \nSuranceBay \nSetting-Up Agents As A Business or LOA \nRule of thumb: in order to process a downline agent in any capacity, the party they are to \npay/report to already needs to be active with that carrier.  \nSome carriers allow for individuals to have LOA’s and others do not.  \nAn agent will always write business as an individual because an agency can’t write business, \nonly earn money from it. Appointment shows who gets the commission. \nLOA means no commissions pay to the agent at all.  Whoever the agent reports to gets the \ncomp and then pays that agent whatever the agreed upon amount is for the sale/product/carrier. \nCommissioned levels mean the agent earns a certain % below street at a level the carrier offers \nformally, and the agent/agency above that agent gets the “override” difference between that \nagents level and street.  \n\nConsider LOA the very bottom of the line. Nothing is below 0 rates. LOA is 0 rates. If you’re \ntalking about the signing officer to the agency, the downline would be under the agency, not the \nofficer themself.  \nLOA definition: \n● The carrier pays all commissions to the agency (not the individual agent). \n \n● The agent is “licensed-only” because they’re not directly contracted with the carrier — \nthey’re working under the agency’s contract. \n \nCommission impact: \n \n● The LOA’s payout is 100% controlled by the agency. \n \n● An LOA can be set below street (common, so the agency keeps an override), or at the \nequivalent of street if the agency decides to pass it all through. \n \n● What they can’t be is above street, since the carrier only pays street to the agency. \n \nChanging from Individual to Business: \n1. Agency EIN established with the IRS. \n2. Agency insurance license for each state you are personally licensed. \n3. Voided check for the agency. \n4. Articles of Incorporation for the agency. \n5. E&O listing agency name, along with your personal name. \n \n \nSetting-Up Sub Agents Underneath an Agent \n- Depends on the carrier - Athene, F&G, Equitrust, and North American have below street \noptions. Allianz does not. Delaware Life is according to product line. The agent must be \nset up as street directly to the carrier. \n- Can an agent set up an LLC and have the agent LOA to the LLC? Depends on the \ncarrier - you need to check with Jamie. I know that Athene and F&G allow this. This also \nmeans that the comp will be sent to the agency and the agent will need to send out \n1099s and handle taxes. \n- If an agent wants sub agents, you will need to let Magellan know when you send over \nthe sub agent contracting paperwork and tell them explicitly what comp schedule they \nshould be set at.  \n\n- The upline agent must hold the same appointments and licenses as the sub agents. \n- How does this type of arrangement work with marketing credits? The principal agent \ndoes not get marketing credits for the override amount. \n- Magellan’s philosophy: We are trying not to entertain the “stacking” of agents.  The most \nsuccessful business model is LOA to an agency.  Our very top practices are a reflection \nof this success. \nComp Grids & Agent Compensation \nhttps://worthlandcapital.app.box.com/folder/56674158595 \n \nCommission grids are different for every carrier, state, and type of enrollment (ICEP, Guarantee \nIssue, etc.). All Invita agents are set up at street compensation. Some of our carriers allow for \nbelow street compensation - agents can build a team with below street comp, but there is no \noverride built in above street. \n \nThat first column is just Allianz's IUL comp sheet is an internal code for what level an agent is at. \nThe first number, 80, is street level and you will see that in option A, your comp is 100 percent. \nAll of our agents are at street level, so level 80 is what you can expect to receive for \ncompensation if you contract with Allianz under Invita. \nReleases \nIf an agent has written business in the last 6 months, and wants to transfer his carrier \nappointment to us, he will need a release. Go here to access the release forms: \nhttps://worthlandcapital.app.box.com/folder/248426248238 \n-    \nIf transferring TO to us, we send the agent the release paperwork, and it must be signed \nby Magellan (and sometimes Invita if there is a spot for an AMFO signature) before sending \nthe release to the agent.\n If the agent wants a release FROM us, his new FMO will provide \nhim with the release paperwork with signatures. The paperwork must be signed and dated by \nboth FMOs and the agent before sending it back to us. Our number for Allianz is 722 \n-  Agents \ndon’t need a release if they haven't written business in 6 months. In a transfer, \nthe new FMO will let the old FMO know the agent has moved. This process typically \ntakes a few days. \n- If an agent was contracted with us at FIG, they will need a release for certain carriers. \nSend the appropriate release and instruct the agent to send it to: \nteamsiglersales@figmarketing.com \n- Corebridge and American Equity \ndoesn’t deal in releases. An agent can appoint under \nas many FMO’s as they wish.  What code they use determines which FMO gets credit.\n \nMost carriers can use Magellan’s general release. However, some carriers require carrier-specific \nreleases. \n\nCarrier’s That Use Carrier-Specific Releases \n Sentinel \n Aspida \n GILICO (for GILICO, Partners Advantage is requesting FMO, but agent will still submit e-apps \nvia Magellan) \n Global Atlantic (the only preappointment state is PA) \n North American \n Allianz (Please mark the Preferred box, Allianz doesn’t do standard annuity anymore. \nMagellan doesn’t need to sign the request, as long as an accepting party has signed \n(Invita) that is who the release agreement is facilitated with. \n Athene (also needs GBS signature) \n American Life \nAllianz Reinstatement Fee \nIf an agent is terminated for non-production, the agent cannot be processed until they have paid \na $100 fee. Instruct the agent to follow these steps: \n \n1. You will need to send in a $100 check before Allianz will accept a new \nappointment request. \n  \n2. Make the check payable to Allianz Life. \n  \n3. In the memo section of your check, write “Agent Reinstatement Fee” \n  \n4. Write a cover letter with your name, ssn and stating you want to reinstate. \n  \n5. Send to: \nAllianz Life Insurance Company                                                                                                     \n5701 Golden Hills Dr \nMinneapolis, MN. 55416 \n  \n6. Send it with a tracking number. \nMagellan Firelight & iPipeline (Life) \niPipeline (Life) \nMost Carriers Life Products are available to send via IGO eApp (iPipeline). The following \ncarriers will be sent via Firelight -  \n● Allianz \n\n● F&G \n● National Western Life  \n \nFor life eapps, use IGO. The agent must set up an account via the VIP portal and they can use \nthe same email they used for Firelight annuities. The account creation will generate an email \nthat will autogenerate the username and password. The agent will use this to login to IGO and \nsubmit apps. If they need to submit a paper app, they can do that under the Forms menu to find \nthe carrier-specific paper app. Email the completed app to life@magellanfinancial.com \n \nErica Cowger is the contact for life apps for our team. ecowger@magellanfinancial.com, or call \n866-779-3553 and ask for Erica. \n \n \nHow to send in paper applications: \nEmail Life as a PDF attachment to life@magellanfinancial.com \nOR \nYou can upload them via the Secure Upload Portal: \nhttps://www.partnerwithmagellan.com/annuity-secure-upload/ \nFirelight (annuities) \n \nThe Firelight team email is bbetts@magellanfinancial.com or  \nannuitybusiness@magellanfinanical.com \n \n\nSample Email Copy for Firelight instructions and Product Training Guide: \nAttached are instructions for how to submit e-apps through Magellan Firelight and complete \ncarrier-specific Product Training. \nIf you are new to e-apps, Firelight is the platform used to submit annuity applications \nelectronically. \nThe attached “How to Access Magellan’s Firelight” will guide you through how to register so you \nare ready to submit your annuity applications. \n*** IMPORTANT*** \n1. If you have used Firelight with a previous FMO/IMO, you will need to use a different \nemail address when accessing Magellan’s Firelight. Otherwise, Firelight could send your \napplication to a previous FMO/IMO. \n  \n2. Please set-up your Firelight account 1-2 days BEFORE you plan on submitting an \nE-app\n. The account approval process on Magellan’s backend is a manual process and it \nmay take a day or two to confirm your account and generate your account confirmation \nemail. Please also check your spam or junk folder, as the account confirmation email is \nsometimes found there. \n  \n3. Bookmark http://www.partnerwithmagellan.com/vip-portal/ so when you are ready to \nwrite business you can easily access Magellan’s Firelight. \n  \n4. Product Training must be completed BEFORE you submit apps through Magellan \nFirelight. See the attached guide for carrier-specific product information and instructions \non how to complete the training. \nHow to send in paper applications: \nEmail annuities as a PDF attachment to annuitybusiness@magellanfinancial.com  \nOR \nYou can upload them via the Secure Upload Portal: \nhttps://www.partnerwithmagellan.com/annuity-secure-upload/ \nFirelight Training \nWhen an agent registers on Firelight, they will receive an email from \nno-reply@firelighteapp.com with the steps to set up their individual login. After the agent has \ncompleted the Firelight application, it will immediately be sent to our queue for review. Magellan \nwill confirm that licensing/training is in compliance and review the application. Once all is \ndeemed in good order, we will approve on our end, and it will be sent to the carrier’s queue for \nprocessing.  \n \n You will receive an email from no-reply@firelighteapp.com with the steps to set up your \nindividual login. The majority of the carriers we represent are available on our platform.  If you \ndon’t see a carrier or product that you are looking for, please let us know.  After you have \n\ncompleted the Firelight application on your end, it will immediately be sent to our queue for \nreview.  We will confirm your licensing/training is in compliance and review the application.  \nOnce all is deemed in good order, we will approve on our end and it will be sent to the carrier’s \nqueue for processing.  Our team will follow up on the application and keep you posted on the \nstatus.  \n  \nIn the meantime, there are training tutorials for various scenarios in Firelight that are extremely \ninformative. The link to view those is \nhttps://www.firelighteapp.com/EGApp/8d96e1fe64d46fde31/Training?tags=Home , OR you can \njust click on the “Training” Tab in Firelight. \n  \nPlease use this browser (save as one of your favorites) to login to Magellan Firelight \nevery time you log in: \nhttps://www.firelighteapp.com/EGAccess/?org=MAG \n  \n** In the event you are an Administrator or Back Office Professional and are submitting \nApplications on behalf of an Advisor(s), please advise PRIOR to Starting Submissions.  \nWe will provide additional instructions to SHARE access for all individuals required** \n  \nPlease let me know if you have any questions or need further explanation on the process.  Our \nteam email is \nannuitybusiness@magellanfinanical.com.  We look forward to working with you! \n \n \nThere are training tutorials for various scenarios in Firelight that are extremely informative: \n \nhttps://www.firelighteapp.com/EGApp/8d96e1fe64d46fde31/Training?tags=Home  \n \nOR  \n \nAgents can click on the “Training” Tab in Firelight. \n \nLogin Troubleshooting Tips \nRia Cruz (Firelight expert) - RCruz@magellanfinancial.com \n \n \n\n \nSomething to be aware of is that sometimes when you access the firelight link incorrectly (either \nfrom your browser that saved it for you or a password manager), it sometimes remembers the \nwrong login page. It’s a finicky system and sometimes web browsers recommend the wrong \nlogin page. \nIt could happen after they do a password reset and firelight accidentally refreshes \nwith the generic login page (the Magellan Firelight page and the generic login page are almost \nidentical). It could also happen if the agent tries to key in the URL manually to pull up the \nfirelight access website. That could also bring up the generic login page.\n \n \nAgents need to clear cookies and caches to get rid of the wrong login page, and then click on \nthe (\nhttps://www.firelighteapp.com/EGAccess/?org=MAG\n) to get into the correct login page \nafter doing that.  \n \n1. Make sure you are using Chrome on a desktop for best compatibility. Mobile devices \nare not ideal for doing password resets. \n \n2. If you are also active using another IMO’s firelight platform, this will definitely cause \ncriss-cross and automatically direct you to their default org when you login. Ideally, \nyou should use different emails for both platforms to avoid criss-cross. ALWAYS log \nout of your firelight session instead of just closing the window. \n \n3. When resetting your password, make sure you are following the password parameters \nyou cannot use any of the previous 3 passwords, The new password must be at \nleast 8 characters, and include 2 numbers and 1 special character. \nPassword reset \nlinks are good for one hour and the hour must pass before trying to reset the \npassword again. After too many failed attempts at incorrect login, your account will get \nlocked and you would need to contact our back office to unlock  \nannuitybusiness@magellanfinancial.com \n \n4. If you are stuck in a loop with password reset not working, the browser is \nremembering the wrong website. At this point, you have no choice but to clear cookies \nand caches on your web browser to resolve this issue. \nClear cookies based on length \nof time that you have been having trouble logging into firelight using the Basic and \nAdvanced tab. \n \n\n \n\na.  \n5. Save this URL as one of your favorites on your web browser: \nhttps://www.firelighteapp.com/EGAccess/?org=MAG\n  \n \nb. Log into the above site using the newly created password. The background \nshould be a light blue and should say “Magellan Login” at the top of the page, \notherwise you are logging into the wrong interface and will get locked out again. \nc. KEY IN the new password and don’t let the password manager enter in the new \npassword. \n \nWhen resetting password: you must not use any of the previous 3 passwords (otherwise the \nsystem will not take it), must have at least 8 characters, 2 numbers and 1 special character. \n(The password reset link is good for one hour) \n  \nPlease make sure you are using this link to log into Firelight after you have reset your password \n(please bookmark for ease of access) \nhttps://www.firelighteapp.com/EGAccess/?org=MAG \n \nDelegate Access \n \n \n \n\nRe-submitting Apps \nYou can “copy” all app paperwork in Firelight to a new e-app package and then send to the \nclient for new signatures and dates. \n \nSteps: \nGo into the application \nGo to Other Actions (top right corner) \nClick “copy activity” \n \nSince new signatures are all that is needed at this time, you can follow the instructions below \nfrom the Firelight Home page in order to copy the application packet within Firelight. This will \nduplicate the original packet information (which you can review for good measure) and be ready \nto send off for the updated signatures. (**Note: any additional documents [statements, \nillustrations, etc.] added to the packet will need to be re-added to the new packet) \n  \n  \nAll Activities >> Copy >> Copy As Is >> then Resubmit after making changes (Signature \nDates, etc.) \n  \n  \n\n \n  \n  \nOnce signed, our teams will get it through the review process and released to the carrier if \neverything looks good. \n \nMedallion Signature Guarantee \nHere is the Medallion Signature Guarantee form that the agent needs to complete and Mail, \n(NOT EMAIL) to Alicia at Magellan. \n  \nHere are the instructions \n  \nMagellan will need the attached form, original/wet ink signature on the transfer form, and a clear \nand visible copy of the client Drivers License mailed to them.  \n  \nMagellan is only able to medallion stamp an original, wet ink signature.  Magellan can not \ncomplete a medallion on a copy that was emailed. \n  \nPut the address of the new carrier you are placing the business with on the form so Magellan \ncan overnight this form and all paperwork to that carrier.   \n  \nMail to the following address: \n  \nMagellan Financial \nAttn: Alicia Padilla \n1315 SW Arrowhead Rd \nTopeka, KS 66604 \n \n\nAnnuity Rate Watch \nJust copy the link below and paste it into your browser. Then you should be able to register. \nhttps://members.annuityratewatch.com/invitafinancial/annuity/ \n \nSo when they open that link there's a button that says Need an Account? Register for free, and that's \nwhere they create their account\n \nIndustry Terms \n- OBA – outside business activity \n- MSA –   If the BD has an MSA with the carrier it doesn’t matter what the agent or the FMO \ntells the carrier, they are legally bound to honor the MSA with the BD. We need \nsomething in writing from the agent so the FMO is not liable for assisting the agent in \nselling away.\n \n- LOE – letter of explanation \n- IGO - In good order \n- NIGO - Not in good order \n- LOA - Licensed-only Agent \n- BD - Broker/Dealer \n \nFAQ \n1. If I transfer my current appointment to Invita, will I lose access to clients I \nnow have under my current FMO? \nWhether or not the agent will retain access to his current clients after the appointment \ntransfer depends on how he is currently setup. If he is street to the current FMO it won't \nbe an issue. If he is assigning his comp to someone or another group he will lose access \nto those clients when he transfers. \n2. Does Magellan or Invita hold compensation for a certain period of time \nbefore paying the agent? \nIf a group that has all their agents comp paid to the group, they have the right to hold \ncomp until the free look period is over. That is not how Magellan operates as we help \nindependent agents and do not take all comp on their behalf. That being said, if a policy \nis above a certain $ amount or commissions are over a certain $ amount the carrier has \nthe right to hold the comp for 30 days.  This would be extreme.\n \n\n3. Do I get an override if I have agent’s underneath me and want to bring them \nover to Invita/Magellan? \n \nNo. All agents are appointed at street level. There is no option for overrides. However, \nthese core carriers allow agents to bring in subagents at levels below street: \nAllianz \nStandard, Equitrust, American Equity, Athene, Nationwide Life, and F&G. Allianz only \noffers street and LOA. To clarify, if the subagent is in the agent’s hierarchy then that \nsubagent will need to be LOA to the agent and the agent would pay the subagent for \nAllianz preferred, the other carriers would be fine to contract because the carrier(s) have \nbelow street level comp. \n4. Can I do E-applications directly on the carrier site, and skip Magellan \nFirelight? \nYes. Please make sure to copy annuitybusiness@magellanfinancial.com \n5. Will my bonus come over with me when I transfer my appointment? \nWith Allianz, bonuses will come over with your agent. Bonus programs follow an agent \nas long as there is no other agency or anything weird going on with how they are setup.\n \n6. When an agent transfers appointments to Invita, will they still be able to \nservice written business with their old FMO? \nWhen new business is issued under an FMO, it remains with that FMO.  The agent still \nhas visibility and servicing, and the trail comp (if applicable) pays out just as the original \npolicy did.\n \n7. Will a pending bankruptcy affect my ability to get contracted? \nMagellan says that background checks are standard each time an agent moves an \nappointment, and a pending bankruptcy can definitely result in a decline and loss of \ncurrent appointment. Carriers generally require a bankruptcy to be 24 months \npost-discharge before allowing an agent to appoint. If a bankruptcy wasn’t due to divorce \nor illness, it will be even harder for this particular agent to make it through backgrounds. \n8. Can an agent get contracted with an outstanding VECTOR? \nEvery carrier is different. Equitrust (and must wait 6 months after debt is paid to \nrecontract) and Athene will not allow it. F&G and Allianz Life WILL allow it if a payment \nplan is submitted. Corebrige allows debts under $2k and no more than two hits. Any \nappeal must be reviewed and approved by management on a case-to-case basis. North \nAmerican, and American Equity? \n\n9. If an agency has two owners, who can be principal? \nOwnership % doesn’t matter as long as whoever they choose needs to be listed as a \nsigning officer on the AOI.  If they are currently appointed, same signing officer needs to \nbe primary with transfer. \n \n10. Do carriers increase comp based on the volume of business? \nSome carriers increase comp based on the volume of business, i.e. Allianz and NOA. \nThe bonus comp added to each policy after the agent hits certain goals would be based \non where the commission is paid. If the agent is simply collecting overrides on the \ndownline business, it won’t help him reach goals quicker. If that downline agent is \nlicensed only to the primary agent, the production would credit the primary agent.  In \nmost cases the primary agent would go on the app for 99% and the downline on it for \n1%. The primary agent would pay the agent his part of the sale but get 99% of the credit \nfor the app/production. \n \n11. Changing Product Set Up Classification \nThere are no specific rules for SureLC, but we require the change to be warranted.  We \ndon’t allow an agent to just change their setup per carrier, per state or for no reason.  It \nresults in re-processing everything and it confuses the carriers. \n \n12. Override \nOverride is simply that, the agent earning the “override” is only entitled to the \ncommissions aspect not the book of business itself.  The agent who met with the client \nand signed the app keeps rights to the business. \n  \nLicensed Only would take this answer in a different direction, but that’s not just \n“override”.  \n \n13. Can you recommend a carrier for E&O insurance? \nMostly I see is CNA or ACORD. You can also send the agent to www.eoforless.com to \nshop for basic coverage, \n  \nNOTICE OF CLAIMS: \nCNA - Claims Reporting \nPO Box 8317, Chicago IL 60680-8317 \nor via email: SpecialtyProNewLoss@cna.com \n \n14. SureLC \nBecause we take on all of the responsibility of processing each carrier appointment for \nthe agent, for compliance purposes and data verification, we do require the forms be \ncompleted & signed.  Misc items such as E&O can be found in SureLC once we get \naccess and if they are missing we can notify you at that time. We use SureLC differently \n\nfrom other FMO, and assume all the appointment responsibility.  The agent will not be \nable to see what we process for him, since it’s us doing it and not him.  Hope that makes \nsense. \n \nYes, we do require the forms for legal reasons.  SureLC is a shared system that all \nFMO’s use so if we don’t’ have paperwork to confirm what the agent wants us to use \nwith the appointments. \n \n If you want to begin using it that way, we can delegate the cost and walk you through \nhow the process would be different entirely if you want your agents doing it that way.  \nThey would be responsible for the information being correct each time and they would \nconfirm their own carrier appointments each time instead of Magellan taking care of all of \nit (which is a service we offer).  Our agents highly prefer the way we do it.  We pay for \nSureLC the same way no matter what. \n \n \n \nLOA is zero rates.  Street is commissions rates, meaning the agent is earning comp from the \ncarrier and NOT assigning it to the agency.  LOA and street rates are very opposite things. \n \nAgency = street \nPrincipal agent = LOA (licensed only agent, which means that street comp is paid to the agency \nfrom a carrier, not to an individual) \nDownline agents under agency = any lower level available or LOA \nIf an agent has a valid EIN and wants to appoint as a business, an agency voided check is \nrequired.  Agents can’t be appointed as Business Entity and have comp deposited into a \npersonal account. \n \n(GBS) - Arthur J. Gallagher Insurance Company   \n \nFYI, we are running a new radio lead offer in which agents can write through us and acquire credits \nfor leads so if they write 300k in a month they get 3k worth of leads (1%) for the next month and this \nis our first agent\n \n \nAttestation form not printing - get blank screen. go in the upper right corner and under the \n“Other Actions” dropdown – choose Display/Print PDF, select the Signature Attestation Form, \nthen print it off that way. \n\n \n \nCouple things to know when submitting an Invita agent to Delaware.  \n1. They are prioritizing agents who have business in hand. \n2. Agents must provide proof of product training from REGED.  They offer it on their portal, \nbut it’s been discovered that the Delaware system isn’t syncing with that portal so \ntraining can’t be found. REGED is the best option and the agent must provide the \ncertificate. \n3. AML is required at the time of appointment submission as well.  \n4. Voided check for individual agents must be in the individual agent name. \n \nDelaware Life \nAmerican Life \nIbexis \nLincoln Life & Prudential for Life - Just in time carrier - need client name and DOB \n\nTransmittal forms go to new business \nannuitybusiness@magellanfinancial.com \nAbc+, 222+ - additional training \n \n \n  \nHello Greg, \n  \n  \nGreat news! Your appointment has been completed.  \nFor us to better meet your needs, please do the following at your earliest convenience: \n  \n● Review your agent code/ appointment information listed below. \n● Please keep this email for your records. \n● Always feel free to reach out to our team for any additional questions or concerns at \ncontracting@invitafinancial.com or contracting@magellanfinancial.com  . \n● Contact our team prior to taking a new business application and we are happy to \ncheck agent compliance for the specific state & product. \n  \nCarrier \nAmerican Life \nStatus \nApproved \nWriting number 31-13192 \nLines of Authority Annuity \nEffective date \n \nWebsite access/Training links Guide Attached \nOutstanding Requirements/Additional \nInformation \n \n  \n  \n  \n\nThank you! \n \n \nIn order to proceed with processing, we kindly require the requested documents. These \ndocuments are specific to the Magellan/Invita onboarding process and necessary to move \nforward and keep files accurately updated to ensure a smooth process when submitting to the \ncarriers. \n \nCarrier Approval Email \n \nHi Justin, \n \nGreat news! Your appointment is now active.  \n \nPlease review your agent code/appointment information listed below, and keep this email for \nyour records.  \n  \nCarrier Allianz Preferred \nStatus Active \nWriting Number 722153995 \nLines of Authority Annuity \nEffective Date 1/8/2026 \nDocuments Product Training Guide \nOutstanding Requirements  \nAdditional Information  \n \nThe product training guide is also attached--this has instructions for how to complete product \ntraining for each carrier.  \n \nLastly, please don't hesitate to reach out to our team before taking a new business application; \nwe are happy to check agent compliance for your specific state and product. \n \nAttachment: PTG \n \nAppointment Requests Email \n \nHello Micah, \n  \nWe have completed your onboarding and have submitted your appointment request to the \nfollowing carriers: \n\n● Allianz Life \n● Allianz Preferred \n● American Equity \n● American Life \n● Athene \n● Equitrust \n● F&G \n● Delaware Life \n● Global Atlantic \n● Ibexis \n● North American \n● Prosperity Life \nAs a reminder, Equitrust requires that you complete product training before they will contract \nyou. I’ve attached the instructions for completing training to this email for your convenience. \n \nWe follow up every 3 business days to check the status of contracting, and once approval is \nreceived, we will notify you. Should you have any questions, please don’t hesitate to ask - I am \nhappy to assist! \n \nWelcome to the team! \n \nAttachment: EQ product training \n \nPaperwork Received Email \n \nHi Austin, \nThank you for sending in your paperwork. Your appointment requests will go out today. \nBefore Equitrust will contract you, you must complete product training and send me the \ncompletion certificate. I've attached the instructions for completing Equitrust product training to \nthis email for easy reference. If you are already contracted with Equitrust, this will be just a \ntransfer request for an existing appointment, and you do not need to complete the training. \nWe will follow up every 3 business days to check the status of contracting and will notify you \nonce we receive your approval for each carrier. Please allow 3-5 business days for carrier \nprocessing. \nAttachment: EQ product training page \nUpdating Carrier w/Docs \n \nPlease update agent’s file: \n  \n\nJames A Crow \nSSN 8888 \nDOB x/xx/xxxx \n \nI just wanted to pass this along to you. Magellan wanted to encourage you to get your business \nlicensed and move forward that way or just as an individual. Checks from Delaware Life would \nbe made out to you individually, and it would be up to your bank's policy if they allow personal \nchecks signed over to a business account to deposit unless your account at the bank is DBA. \nThey suggested you reach out to your bank and see how the account is titled and if they allow \nthat before moving that route.  \n ",
    "url": null,
    "content": null,
    "addedAt": "2026-03-06T00:39:04.469Z"
  },
  {
    "id": "1772757834147",
    "name": "Dinner and movie training.pdf",
    "type": "upload",
    "fileName": "Dinner and movie training.pdf",
    "filePath": "/Users/brettkitchen/AI VOICE IMPRINT/kb-widget/uploads/1772757833961-Dinner and movie training.pdf",
    "extractedText": "\n\nDinner and movie training \n \nWEBVTT \n \n1 \n00:00:47.175 --> 00:00:48.195 \nHey, Brett Kitchen here \n \n2 \n00:00:48.195 --> 00:00:51.355 \nand welcome to our Retirement Everest \n \n3 \n00:00:51.415 --> 00:00:52.555 \ndinner and movie program. \n \n4 \n00:00:53.615 --> 00:00:55.515 \nAnd of course it includes all of our movies. \n \n5 \n00:00:55.605 --> 00:00:58.715 \nWe've got Retirement Deception, we've got social insecurity, \n \n6 \n00:00:58.885 --> 00:01:01.635 \nwe've got a takeoff on the Retirement Deception, \n \n7 \n00:01:01.635 --> 00:01:02.915 \nwhich is a shorter film. \n \n8 \n00:01:03.295 --> 00:01:05.035 \nWe call it License to Spend. \n \n9 \n00:01:05.545 --> 00:01:08.155 \nThat keeps the, the messaging a little fresh. \n \n10 \n00:01:08.655 --> 00:01:11.795 \nAnd we've got multiple new ones coming out, um, \n \n\n11 \n00:01:11.805 --> 00:01:13.035 \nthroughout the next year. \n \n12 \n00:01:13.455 --> 00:01:15.595 \nSo we always want to keep more content, \n \n13 \n00:01:15.685 --> 00:01:17.115 \nfresh content coming your way. \n \n14 \n00:01:17.255 --> 00:01:19.675 \nSo, uh, we're excited to have you involved. \n \n15 \n00:01:20.065 --> 00:01:23.115 \nThis particular video is going to be a consolidation \n \n16 \n00:01:23.115 --> 00:01:24.915 \nof the entire training so \n \n17 \n00:01:25.035 --> 00:01:27.395 \nthat you can get everything you need right away, \n \n18 \n00:01:27.415 --> 00:01:30.435 \nand then you can go through each step of the training \n \n19 \n00:01:30.455 --> 00:01:32.275 \nand get more detail if you'd like to do that. \n \n20 \n00:01:32.855 --> 00:01:35.155 \nUm, and I just wanna highlight that, uh, \n \n21 \n00:01:35.185 --> 00:01:38.195 \nthis working really well for, for our producers. \n \n\n22 \n00:01:38.245 --> 00:01:40.995 \nWe've had, uh, multiple events just last night, \n \n23 \n00:01:41.615 --> 00:01:45.075 \ngot a picture in from one of them here on the screen that, \n \n24 \n00:01:45.175 --> 00:01:46.715 \nuh, we had a two night event. \n \n25 \n00:01:46.775 --> 00:01:50.675 \nWe had 56 attendees spread over those two nights about, uh, \n \n26 \n00:01:50.895 --> 00:01:54.315 \nyou know, 39, 38 buying units \n \n27 \n00:01:54.935 --> 00:01:58.675 \nand ended up with 18 appointments, which is just awesome \n \n28 \n00:01:58.675 --> 00:02:00.915 \nbecause these are people that have watched the movie. \n \n29 \n00:02:01.075 --> 00:02:03.355 \nI mean, these are people that are qualified \n \n30 \n00:02:03.415 --> 00:02:04.635 \nand are sold on annuities, \n \n31 \n00:02:04.635 --> 00:02:08.155 \nand now they wanna meet with our producers and then do that. \n \n32 \n00:02:08.225 --> 00:02:09.835 \nGreg just shot me a text. \n \n\n33 \n00:02:10.255 --> 00:02:12.195 \nHis event last night had 30 people there, \n \n34 \n00:02:12.855 --> 00:02:13.995 \n10 discovery visits. \n \n35 \n00:02:14.135 --> 00:02:16.115 \nAnd when you, when you back that out, you know, \n \n36 \n00:02:16.115 --> 00:02:19.075 \nwhen you got 30 people, you're probably somewhere \n \n37 \n00:02:19.075 --> 00:02:22.715 \naround 22, 20 to 22, uh, buying units. \n \n38 \n00:02:22.895 --> 00:02:24.035 \nAnd that's what we're shooting for, \n \n39 \n00:02:24.415 --> 00:02:27.275 \nto hit 50% mark of the buying units. \n \n40 \n00:02:27.775 --> 00:02:30.155 \nAnd so you'll know if you're doing well, if you're above \n \n41 \n00:02:30.155 --> 00:02:32.715 \nthat, and if you're below that, we know we need to dial \n \n42 \n00:02:32.715 --> 00:02:34.515 \nthat in and we'll give you the scripts \n \n43 \n00:02:34.535 --> 00:02:35.595 \nand everything to do that with. \n \n\n44 \n00:02:35.735 --> 00:02:38.995 \nSo, uh, just wanted to highlight, you know, just \n \n45 \n00:02:38.995 --> 00:02:42.835 \nhow exciting it is to see these events produce full rooms, \n \n46 \n00:02:43.295 --> 00:02:46.435 \npeople that want to meet with you, cases that close. \n \n47 \n00:02:47.055 --> 00:02:50.035 \nUh, one of our advisors just chatted in. \n \n48 \n00:02:50.035 --> 00:02:52.115 \nHe, he wrote 200,000 from his event. \n \n49 \n00:02:52.345 --> 00:02:54.995 \nGreg just wrote 300,000 today on one of his events. \n \n50 \n00:02:55.495 --> 00:02:57.875 \nAnd so, uh, it's, it's fun. \n \n51 \n00:02:58.175 --> 00:03:01.005 \nThe, the events are really working. \n \n52 \n00:03:01.235 --> 00:03:02.605 \nThey're changing people's lives, \n \n53 \n00:03:02.605 --> 00:03:04.805 \nthey're changing our producer's lives, \n \n54 \n00:03:05.385 --> 00:03:08.245 \nand we're getting a consistent flow of business. \n \n\n55 \n00:03:08.365 --> 00:03:12.245 \nI mean, Jeremy Sparks wrote 1.5 million in a single week. \n \n56 \n00:03:12.245 --> 00:03:15.925 \nThat's over 105 grand in a, in a week for commissions. \n \n57 \n00:03:16.305 --> 00:03:19.925 \nAnd Dustin wrote 4 million in a month recently. \n \n58 \n00:03:20.305 --> 00:03:21.925 \nSo you wanna talk about life changing. \n \n59 \n00:03:22.085 --> 00:03:24.565 \nI mean, that's almost 300 grand in commission \n \n60 \n00:03:25.025 --> 00:03:26.045 \nin just one month. \n \n61 \n00:03:26.185 --> 00:03:28.165 \nSo really cool stuff's happening. \n \n62 \n00:03:28.225 --> 00:03:30.005 \nWe want you to be successful with this. \n \n63 \n00:03:30.015 --> 00:03:31.565 \nWe're happy to be have you involved. \n \n64 \n00:03:31.905 --> 00:03:33.725 \nAnd one other thing, if, if you're not aware \n \n65 \n00:03:33.725 --> 00:03:36.325 \nof our $10 million blueprint, uh, program, \n \n\n66 \n00:03:36.705 --> 00:03:38.005 \nI'm gonna link to that below. \n \n67 \n00:03:38.165 --> 00:03:39.845 \n'cause that's how we really blow up your business. \n \n68 \n00:03:39.845 --> 00:03:42.365 \nThat's how we really help you add millions \n \n69 \n00:03:42.365 --> 00:03:43.845 \nand millions of dollars in production. \n \n70 \n00:03:44.265 --> 00:03:47.525 \nAnd there's additional assets that we will create for you \n \n71 \n00:03:47.875 --> 00:03:50.845 \nthat cost you nothing other than a commitment \n \n72 \n00:03:50.845 --> 00:03:52.845 \nto really go in and be partners together. \n \n73 \n00:03:52.905 --> 00:03:54.285 \nSo check that out after you're \n \n74 \n00:03:54.285 --> 00:03:55.405 \ndone going through this training. \n \n75 \n00:03:57.635 --> 00:04:00.055 \nAll right, welcome to this fast start \n \n76 \n00:04:00.435 --> 00:04:01.615 \ndinner and a movie training. \n \n\n77 \n00:04:01.725 --> 00:04:04.415 \nThere's gonna be more details in the subsequent videos. \n \n78 \n00:04:04.935 --> 00:04:08.015 \nI wanted to give you everything that you needed in one shot. \n \n79 \n00:04:08.435 --> 00:04:10.215 \nSo if you just want to take it all in, get going \n \n80 \n00:04:10.435 --> 00:04:12.055 \nand get started, then that you can do \n \n81 \n00:04:12.055 --> 00:04:14.725 \nthat here in this one video. \n \n82 \n00:04:14.725 --> 00:04:16.445 \nGetting the global idea \n \n83 \n00:04:16.545 --> 00:04:18.285 \nof everything you need to know to get going. \n \n84 \n00:04:18.385 --> 00:04:20.605 \nSo first of all, dinner and a movie overview. \n \n85 \n00:04:21.065 --> 00:04:22.445 \nWe found that this works really well \n \n86 \n00:04:22.445 --> 00:04:25.285 \nbecause it's a combination of an offer that is enticing, \n \n87 \n00:04:25.355 --> 00:04:26.725 \nit's got value, high value, \n \n\n88 \n00:04:26.725 --> 00:04:29.085 \nand it's different, uh, than what they're seeing out there. \n \n89 \n00:04:29.305 --> 00:04:33.565 \nAnd steak dinner workshops and, you know, social security \n \n90 \n00:04:33.665 --> 00:04:35.765 \nor state planning or everything else that that's out there. \n \n91 \n00:04:36.225 --> 00:04:38.605 \nAnd so, um, first of all, the dinner \n \n92 \n00:04:38.605 --> 00:04:41.445 \nand a movie idea being one that you can do in theaters. \n \n93 \n00:04:41.705 --> 00:04:44.725 \nYou can cater food in, you can use the theaters \n \n94 \n00:04:44.725 --> 00:04:46.045 \nthat actually have a kitchen, \n \n95 \n00:04:46.465 --> 00:04:48.045 \nor you can do it in restaurants \n \n96 \n00:04:48.545 --> 00:04:51.245 \nand other venues where you can actually provide dinner. \n \n97 \n00:04:51.705 --> 00:04:54.005 \nUh, some of those venues could be like gathering places, \n \n98 \n00:04:54.195 --> 00:04:55.645 \ncountry clubs, things like that. \n \n\n99 \n00:04:55.825 --> 00:04:58.165 \nBut in general, you don't want to be doing these in like, \n \n100 \n00:04:58.165 --> 00:05:00.005 \nsome obscure workshop like library \n \n101 \n00:05:00.225 --> 00:05:02.205 \nand bringing in cold pizza, right? \n \n102 \n00:05:02.205 --> 00:05:05.405 \nThat's not the idea. Uh, couple of questions that come along \n \n103 \n00:05:05.405 --> 00:05:07.605 \nwith this is the age old. \n \n104 \n00:05:07.885 --> 00:05:09.485 \nI hate giving people dinner. \n \n105 \n00:05:09.925 --> 00:05:12.405 \nI hate giving people food because I get plate lickers. \n \n106 \n00:05:13.105 --> 00:05:17.405 \nAnd my response to that is, I understand nobody likes \n \n107 \n00:05:17.405 --> 00:05:18.565 \nto be taken advantage of. \n \n108 \n00:05:18.955 --> 00:05:20.885 \nHowever, in this business, \n \n109 \n00:05:20.935 --> 00:05:23.085 \nthere is a cost of doing business. \n \n\n110 \n00:05:23.745 --> 00:05:26.845 \nAnd if you want to make money, then you have \n \n111 \n00:05:26.845 --> 00:05:30.605 \nto do what's required to get people to attend your events. \n \n112 \n00:05:31.145 --> 00:05:34.685 \nAnd what's required right now is delivering them food. \n \n113 \n00:05:35.345 --> 00:05:37.405 \nAnd so you can do other workshops \n \n114 \n00:05:37.405 --> 00:05:39.485 \nand you can see I just literally got off the phone \n \n115 \n00:05:39.485 --> 00:05:42.165 \nwith a guy today who had five people at his workshop, \n \n116 \n00:05:43.145 --> 00:05:44.245 \nif that's what you're after. \n \n117 \n00:05:44.865 --> 00:05:49.165 \nHe did all the marketing, did the workshop, two PE two, \n \n118 \n00:05:49.165 --> 00:05:52.365 \nfive people there, two ments for all of that build up \n \n119 \n00:05:52.365 --> 00:05:54.125 \nand work and time and effort. \n \n120 \n00:05:54.705 --> 00:05:56.485 \nUm, that's not what we're after. \n \n\n121 \n00:05:57.015 --> 00:05:59.045 \nWe're after filling an event with 30 people \n \n122 \n00:05:59.145 --> 00:06:02.245 \nand walking outta there with 8, 10, 12 appointments. \n \n123 \n00:06:02.905 --> 00:06:07.045 \nAnd so, uh, the, the simple, I guess \n \n124 \n00:06:07.865 --> 00:06:12.605 \nanswer to all of this is get over the frustration \n \n125 \n00:06:12.825 --> 00:06:17.675 \nof giving away free meals with the potential \n \n126 \n00:06:17.695 --> 00:06:20.755 \nand the income of actually writing a bunch of business. \n \n127 \n00:06:21.705 --> 00:06:24.365 \nSo use the upside opportunity \n \n128 \n00:06:25.145 --> 00:06:26.845 \nof writing half a million \n \n129 \n00:06:27.105 --> 00:06:30.605 \nor a million dollars to overcome the frustration \n \n130 \n00:06:30.605 --> 00:06:32.645 \nand anger that you feel when somebody tries \n \n131 \n00:06:32.645 --> 00:06:34.645 \nto take advantage of your free food. \n \n\n132 \n00:06:35.305 --> 00:06:38.765 \nUh, one of our agents has a totally different take on it. \n \n133 \n00:06:38.825 --> 00:06:40.125 \nShe is an abundance mindset \n \n134 \n00:06:40.225 --> 00:06:42.085 \nand she's like, if I have to feed a few people \n \n135 \n00:06:42.395 --> 00:06:44.765 \nbecause they're hungry, I'm happy to do it. \n \n136 \n00:06:45.185 --> 00:06:47.485 \nAnd people just can't get outta their own way on this. \n \n137 \n00:06:48.065 --> 00:06:50.725 \nUh, and so we get questions like, what if I don't want \n \n138 \n00:06:50.725 --> 00:06:52.245 \nto feed a full meal? \n \n139 \n00:06:52.245 --> 00:06:54.605 \nWhat if I just wanna do refreshments? Is that okay? \n \n140 \n00:06:54.785 --> 00:06:59.125 \nAnd if you are unwilling to pay the price of admission, \n \n141 \n00:06:59.475 --> 00:07:00.765 \nthen don't enter. \n \n142 \n00:07:01.845 --> 00:07:05.065 \nDo not enter the theme park if you don't want \n \n\n143 \n00:07:05.065 --> 00:07:06.065 \nto pay the price of admission. \n \n144 \n00:07:06.935 --> 00:07:08.115 \nAnd so that's what it is. \n \n145 \n00:07:08.525 --> 00:07:12.945 \nWe're not gonna do light refreshments, coffee and cookies, \n \n146 \n00:07:13.005 --> 00:07:14.145 \nand I hope people show up. \n \n147 \n00:07:14.795 --> 00:07:16.305 \nWe're going to be economical, \n \n148 \n00:07:16.305 --> 00:07:18.305 \nwe're gonna show you the best way to do it so \n \n149 \n00:07:18.305 --> 00:07:19.665 \nthat it doesn't cost you near as much \n \n150 \n00:07:19.665 --> 00:07:21.745 \nas it is in other formats. \n \n151 \n00:07:22.325 --> 00:07:24.505 \nBut there is no getting around the fact \n \n152 \n00:07:24.505 --> 00:07:27.065 \nthat if you want maximum show up rates, then you're, \n \n153 \n00:07:27.065 --> 00:07:28.105 \nyou're gonna offer meals \n \n\n154 \n00:07:28.165 --> 00:07:30.585 \nand you're gonna show them that they're gonna get fed. \n \n155 \n00:07:31.495 --> 00:07:35.465 \nOkay? So that's on my rant on that. \n \n156 \n00:07:35.465 --> 00:07:38.545 \nAnd, and i, it comes up so often that obviously I have \n \n157 \n00:07:38.545 --> 00:07:40.185 \nto hit it right, right outta the gate. \n \n158 \n00:07:41.025 --> 00:07:42.905 \nProbably wouldn't have signed up if you were totally opposed \n \n159 \n00:07:42.905 --> 00:07:45.145 \nto it, but we still get people to sign up for the dinner \n \n160 \n00:07:45.145 --> 00:07:46.345 \nand movie program and then say, \n \n161 \n00:07:46.545 --> 00:07:47.585 \nI don't want to feed anybody. \n \n162 \n00:07:48.405 --> 00:07:50.545 \nAnd that's just, it's just not gonna happen. \n \n163 \n00:07:50.765 --> 00:07:54.185 \nSo let's jump in, let's select the venues, the dates, \n \n164 \n00:07:54.485 --> 00:07:55.905 \net cetera, get a lot of questions. \n \n\n165 \n00:07:55.905 --> 00:07:57.865 \nCan I do this lunch? Can I do it on the weekends? \n \n166 \n00:07:58.005 --> 00:07:59.665 \nAnd I'm just gonna tell you what works the best. \n \n167 \n00:07:59.665 --> 00:08:02.905 \nAnd I want you to just trust us on this. \n \n168 \n00:08:03.255 --> 00:08:04.905 \nOnce you're writing a million bucks a month \n \n169 \n00:08:05.045 --> 00:08:06.425 \nand you wanna start fooling around \n \n170 \n00:08:06.425 --> 00:08:08.905 \nand changing the recipe, be my guest, \n \n171 \n00:08:09.325 --> 00:08:10.985 \nbut forget for starters. \n \n172 \n00:08:10.985 --> 00:08:12.785 \nJust do what works. And that's a Tuesday \n \n173 \n00:08:13.005 --> 00:08:14.625 \nor a Thursday night, Tuesday, Wednesday, \n \n174 \n00:08:14.825 --> 00:08:19.225 \nThursday night event, uh, with, at a restaurant that has a, \n \n175 \n00:08:19.745 --> 00:08:23.105 \nI would say B2B plus to a minus level restaurant. \n \n\n176 \n00:08:23.395 --> 00:08:24.465 \nWe're not doing a plus. \n \n177 \n00:08:24.595 --> 00:08:26.665 \nWe're not doing Flemings and Rus Chris \n \n178 \n00:08:26.965 --> 00:08:29.505 \nand the high end 80, 90, a hundred dollars plates. \n \n179 \n00:08:29.505 --> 00:08:31.025 \nWe're doing $30 plates. \n \n180 \n00:08:31.235 --> 00:08:35.145 \nWe're doing it in a buffet format, which is less expensive \n \n181 \n00:08:35.165 --> 00:08:37.785 \nfor everybody and it allows you \n \n182 \n00:08:37.845 --> 00:08:40.225 \nto control the environment the way that it needs \n \n183 \n00:08:40.225 --> 00:08:41.905 \nto be controlled for you to be successful at this. \n \n184 \n00:08:42.165 --> 00:08:46.585 \nSo Red Lobster, uh, Texas Roadhouse, \n \n185 \n00:08:47.055 --> 00:08:50.225 \nMexican restaurants that are nice Italian restaurants \n \n186 \n00:08:50.225 --> 00:08:52.305 \nthat are nice, those can all work that have worked. \n \n\n187 \n00:08:52.685 --> 00:08:55.365 \nI'm not saying go to Golden Corral. I'm not saying that. \n \n188 \n00:08:55.825 --> 00:08:58.045 \nI'm saying when you go, you find a b to \n \n189 \n00:08:58.185 --> 00:09:01.565 \nto a minus level restaurant that's popular in your area \n \n190 \n00:09:01.795 --> 00:09:03.205 \nthat has a room that we can use. \n \n191 \n00:09:03.585 --> 00:09:05.085 \nAnd that's what we're gonna wanna do. \n \n192 \n00:09:05.105 --> 00:09:06.605 \nAnd we're gonna wanna do it buffet style \n \n193 \n00:09:06.765 --> 00:09:07.845 \n'cause it's cheaper than Plated \n \n194 \n00:09:08.145 --> 00:09:09.565 \nand you can control the menu. \n \n195 \n00:09:10.105 --> 00:09:12.845 \nSo, um, can you do two, two nights? \n \n196 \n00:09:12.905 --> 00:09:14.605 \nYes, you can, you can do two nights \n \n197 \n00:09:14.605 --> 00:09:17.085 \nbecause you can, you can market twice as much \n \n\n198 \n00:09:17.085 --> 00:09:18.365 \nand fill two nights in the same week. \n \n199 \n00:09:18.365 --> 00:09:19.885 \nSo it's Tuesday, Thursday, that's fine. \n \n200 \n00:09:20.225 --> 00:09:21.805 \nIf you have a direct mail drop \n \n201 \n00:09:21.905 --> 00:09:24.285 \nand you get more than you were anticipating, \n \n202 \n00:09:24.305 --> 00:09:25.525 \nyou get add a second night \n \n203 \n00:09:25.525 --> 00:09:26.925 \nand then you can backfill that with digital. \n \n204 \n00:09:27.465 --> 00:09:30.085 \nAnd so, uh, that is what I recommend. \n \n205 \n00:09:30.175 --> 00:09:31.845 \nLunch does not perform as well. \n \n206 \n00:09:32.625 --> 00:09:36.285 \nUh, weekends we have a few tested that have done that \n \n207 \n00:09:36.465 --> 00:09:39.805 \nand I, I don't have enough data to say that yes, \n \n208 \n00:09:39.805 --> 00:09:43.765 \nthis is a proven winner, so don't do it, just do Tuesdays, \n \n\n209 \n00:09:43.815 --> 00:09:45.605 \nWednesdays, Thursdays, one \n \n210 \n00:09:45.605 --> 00:09:48.765 \nor two nights in a, in a given week and run it. \n \n211 \n00:09:49.015 --> 00:09:50.525 \nWrite business, make money \n \n212 \n00:09:51.065 --> 00:09:54.085 \nand don't let yourself get in your own way eventually. \n \n213 \n00:09:54.085 --> 00:09:55.565 \nLike I said, you can fool around \n \n214 \n00:09:55.565 --> 00:09:56.725 \nwith whatever you want when you're \n \n215 \n00:09:56.725 --> 00:09:57.885 \nwriting a million or two a month. \n \n216 \n00:09:58.265 --> 00:10:01.965 \nBut for now, for for what we know works is what works. \n \n217 \n00:10:02.465 --> 00:10:03.805 \nNow let's talk about the marketing. \n \n218 \n00:10:03.995 --> 00:10:05.525 \nThere's a couple of different ways to do this. \n \n219 \n00:10:05.525 --> 00:10:07.565 \nThere's direct mail, there's Facebook ads. \n \n\n220 \n00:10:07.985 --> 00:10:09.885 \nThe cost disparity is, is high. \n \n221 \n00:10:09.885 --> 00:10:12.045 \nDirect mail is gonna be probably about twice as much. \n \n222 \n00:10:12.045 --> 00:10:13.725 \nYou're gonna get a higher quality of person, \n \n223 \n00:10:13.725 --> 00:10:15.965 \nyou're gonna get a higher show up rate with direct map. \n \n224 \n00:10:16.105 --> 00:10:18.245 \nSo if you've got a larger budget, direct mail is great. \n \n225 \n00:10:18.545 --> 00:10:19.685 \nYou can send out a mailer. \n \n226 \n00:10:19.685 --> 00:10:22.005 \nAnd then even still, if that doesn't fill your event, \n \n227 \n00:10:22.025 --> 00:10:23.285 \nyou can backfill with digital. \n \n228 \n00:10:23.985 --> 00:10:28.045 \nUm, we have been able to improve our digital marketing \n \n229 \n00:10:28.045 --> 00:10:30.365 \nthrough what we call a a three filtering process, \n \n230 \n00:10:30.815 --> 00:10:33.765 \nwhere we are filtering based on exclusionary \n \n\n231 \n00:10:33.765 --> 00:10:35.245 \ninformation that we upload. \n \n232 \n00:10:35.265 --> 00:10:36.565 \nWe buy from data companies \n \n233 \n00:10:36.565 --> 00:10:38.045 \nand say, we don't want these people \n \n234 \n00:10:38.275 --> 00:10:39.605 \nexclude them from our list. \n \n235 \n00:10:40.025 --> 00:10:44.365 \nWe do targeting from that same list, 55, 58 years old, plus \n \n236 \n00:10:44.465 --> 00:10:48.285 \nto 74 with assets, income, house home values, \n \n237 \n00:10:48.505 --> 00:10:51.645 \nand a multitude of other characteristics that show us \n \n238 \n00:10:51.745 --> 00:10:53.765 \nwho our ideal target is. \n \n239 \n00:10:53.865 --> 00:10:55.725 \nAnd we upload them into the campaign. \n \n240 \n00:10:56.225 --> 00:10:59.045 \nAnd we've been training our pixel for the past two years on \n \n241 \n00:10:59.045 --> 00:11:00.205 \nwho we want and who we don't want. \n \n\n242 \n00:11:00.205 --> 00:11:03.245 \nAnd the pixel is the algorithm that meta is using \n \n243 \n00:11:03.265 --> 00:11:06.405 \nto figure out who should I send to you? \n \n244 \n00:11:06.985 --> 00:11:09.085 \nAnd we say, don't send us this group of people. \n \n245 \n00:11:09.235 --> 00:11:11.845 \nSend us this group. And that continues to refine \n \n246 \n00:11:11.845 --> 00:11:13.005 \nand get better and better over time. \n \n247 \n00:11:13.265 --> 00:11:14.565 \nSo those are the two first things. \n \n248 \n00:11:14.565 --> 00:11:16.685 \nAnd then the third is the actual messaging. \n \n249 \n00:11:17.185 --> 00:11:20.845 \nHow do I, uh, what ads do we put out there \n \n250 \n00:11:20.845 --> 00:11:25.085 \nthat actually show the, the folks that you know, this is \n \n251 \n00:11:25.085 --> 00:11:26.245 \nwhat they're after and, and, \n \n252 \n00:11:26.305 --> 00:11:29.525 \nand kind of grab their attention to the point where they're, \n \n\n253 \n00:11:29.525 --> 00:11:32.005 \nthey're pulled in and they want to, uh, \n \n254 \n00:11:32.545 --> 00:11:34.285 \ngo watch something that appeals to them. \n \n255 \n00:11:34.665 --> 00:11:36.325 \nAnd so I'm gonna pull this up real quick \n \n256 \n00:11:36.545 --> 00:11:39.725 \nand show you some screenshots of these ads so \n \n257 \n00:11:39.725 --> 00:11:41.565 \nthat you can get a feel for what we're doing. \n \n258 \n00:11:42.145 --> 00:11:44.605 \nUm, some people say, did the ads show dinner? \n \n259 \n00:11:44.825 --> 00:11:46.565 \nDo they show the food? Of course they do. \n \n260 \n00:11:47.315 --> 00:11:50.685 \nLike it's all about the offer, right? It's the offer stupid. \n \n261 \n00:11:51.305 --> 00:11:55.965 \nAnd, um, so we have to have an offer that actually pulls. \n \n262 \n00:11:56.665 --> 00:12:01.465 \nAnd so here are some of the ads we, uh, \n \n263 \n00:12:01.485 --> 00:12:04.705 \nget some of the, the text copy that we have in here. \n \n\n264 \n00:12:06.595 --> 00:12:08.655 \nUm, if you've saved over 150,000 \n \n265 \n00:12:08.655 --> 00:12:10.615 \nfor retirement, what makes this different? \n \n266 \n00:12:10.615 --> 00:12:12.175 \nThis isn't typical financial seminar. \n \n267 \n00:12:12.315 --> 00:12:13.775 \nYou enjoy complimentary dinner \n \n268 \n00:12:13.775 --> 00:12:15.215 \nwhile watching retirement Everest. \n \n269 \n00:12:15.275 --> 00:12:17.575 \nYou'll discover how to mathematically maximize \n \n270 \n00:12:17.575 --> 00:12:18.735 \nwhat you can spend in retirement. \n \n271 \n00:12:18.735 --> 00:12:20.775 \nFive bit biggest retirement dangers, how \n \n272 \n00:12:20.775 --> 00:12:22.695 \nto walk in your lifestyle for life strategies \n \n273 \n00:12:22.695 --> 00:12:24.255 \nto inflation proof your income. \n \n274 \n00:12:24.515 --> 00:12:26.775 \nYou'll meet the happiest retirees you've ever seen. \n \n\n275 \n00:12:26.775 --> 00:12:27.855 \nPeople have solved these problems \n \n276 \n00:12:27.855 --> 00:12:30.055 \nand no longer lose sleep over their financial future. \n \n277 \n00:12:30.715 --> 00:12:32.775 \nSo we get some of that and we get some \n \n278 \n00:12:32.775 --> 00:12:34.615 \nof these types of images. \n \n279 \n00:12:35.075 --> 00:12:37.215 \nThe reality is this stuff is changing all the time. \n \n280 \n00:12:37.635 --> 00:12:38.935 \nSo whenever you're watching this, \n \n281 \n00:12:38.995 --> 00:12:41.175 \nyou can pretty much be guaranteed that some \n \n282 \n00:12:41.175 --> 00:12:42.775 \nof this has changed, uh, \n \n283 \n00:12:42.775 --> 00:12:45.655 \nbecause you just have to rotate out the, \n \n284 \n00:12:45.835 --> 00:12:47.295 \nthe creative in order to keep it working. \n \n285 \n00:12:48.035 --> 00:12:52.175 \nUh, so that's digital. Direct mail is a whole nother animal. \n \n\n286 \n00:12:52.195 --> 00:12:55.135 \nAnd I'll give you a link to our direct mail, uh, partner \n \n287 \n00:12:55.475 --> 00:12:57.655 \nand his name's Donnie and I can talk you through that. \n \n288 \n00:12:57.815 --> 00:12:59.655 \nI highly recommend it exploring that \n \n289 \n00:12:59.655 --> 00:13:01.095 \nbecause the direct mail does work \n \n290 \n00:13:01.115 --> 00:13:02.375 \nand it pulls very good people. \n \n291 \n00:13:03.035 --> 00:13:07.815 \nUm, and so, um, that's something else you can explore \n \n292 \n00:13:07.995 --> 00:13:09.415 \nand, and do both, right? \n \n293 \n00:13:09.575 --> 00:13:12.735 \nThis isn't an either or. It's a and we want to do both. \n \n294 \n00:13:12.835 --> 00:13:14.335 \nWe want to keep this message fresh. \n \n295 \n00:13:14.395 --> 00:13:15.855 \nWe want to be able to continue \n \n296 \n00:13:16.155 --> 00:13:18.255 \nto do multiple events a month in your area. \n \n\n297 \n00:13:18.955 --> 00:13:21.015 \nUh, okay, can you target zip codes? \n \n298 \n00:13:21.075 --> 00:13:24.015 \nWe don't target zip codes, we do radiuses around the event \n \n299 \n00:13:24.115 --> 00:13:26.815 \nand we do all of our filtering through the, uh, \n \n300 \n00:13:26.815 --> 00:13:28.975 \ndifferent types of exclusions \n \n301 \n00:13:29.195 --> 00:13:30.935 \nand targeting that we've already talked about. \n \n302 \n00:13:31.635 --> 00:13:33.495 \nUm, how do we get the right age range? \n \n303 \n00:13:33.745 --> 00:13:35.295 \nThose are part of the lists \n \n304 \n00:13:35.295 --> 00:13:36.535 \nthat we buy from the data company. \n \n305 \n00:13:36.595 --> 00:13:37.855 \nIt includes age ranges \n \n306 \n00:13:38.115 --> 00:13:41.335 \nand then there is some age related filtering that happens \n \n307 \n00:13:42.075 --> 00:13:44.375 \nin the Facebook platform, although it's not very good \n \n\n308 \n00:13:44.975 --> 00:13:46.415 \n'cause they've taken a lot of that away. \n \n309 \n00:13:46.795 --> 00:13:49.295 \nAll right, so once you've chosen the, the venue, \n \n310 \n00:13:49.395 --> 00:13:50.935 \nand let me go back to that real quickly. \n \n311 \n00:13:51.835 --> 00:13:55.415 \nUh, you, you probably should start \n \n312 \n00:13:55.415 --> 00:13:57.135 \nwith a restaurant at some point. \n \n313 \n00:13:57.675 --> 00:13:59.895 \nGo do a theater. But if you're doing kind \n \n314 \n00:13:59.895 --> 00:14:02.335 \nof just getting off the ground or restaurant is a good venue \n \n315 \n00:14:02.335 --> 00:14:05.535 \nbecause it's less people, there's more interaction \n \n316 \n00:14:05.595 --> 00:14:07.375 \nand more opportunity to, \n \n317 \n00:14:08.115 --> 00:14:10.455 \nto make the most outta every visitor that you have. \n \n318 \n00:14:10.725 --> 00:14:12.335 \nOnce you get into an auditorium, \n \n\n319 \n00:14:12.355 --> 00:14:14.375 \nyou've got 60, 70, 80 people there. \n \n320 \n00:14:14.995 --> 00:14:18.135 \nThat's a lot more logistics and it's great \n \n321 \n00:14:18.415 --> 00:14:20.655 \n'cause you can set 20, 30 appointments in a night, \n \n322 \n00:14:20.675 --> 00:14:22.095 \nbut you gotta have a little bit more staff \n \n323 \n00:14:22.155 --> 00:14:24.215 \nand it's just a little bit more challenging. \n \n324 \n00:14:24.975 --> 00:14:27.795 \nUh, so number five, confirmation. How do you do it? \n \n325 \n00:14:27.895 --> 00:14:29.675 \nUh, you, you just have to pay attention \n \n326 \n00:14:29.675 --> 00:14:31.395 \nto when your leads come in and confirm that. \n \n327 \n00:14:31.575 --> 00:14:34.475 \nUh, we have the drip follow up emails that are going out, \n \n328 \n00:14:34.535 --> 00:14:37.195 \nthe confirmation emails of part of your producer autopilot. \n \n329 \n00:14:37.305 --> 00:14:39.395 \nMake sure you logged in to producer Autopilot will make sure \n \n\n330 \n00:14:39.395 --> 00:14:42.235 \nyou download the app called Lead Connector so \n \n331 \n00:14:42.315 --> 00:14:44.995 \nthat every time an a lead comes in, it pops up \n \n332 \n00:14:44.995 --> 00:14:46.715 \nand says, Hey, you usually got a new registration. \n \n333 \n00:14:46.715 --> 00:14:48.915 \nAnd it's like, okay, awesome. Let me make sure \n \n334 \n00:14:49.025 --> 00:14:51.765 \nthat I respond to these folks when they text back \n \n335 \n00:14:51.785 --> 00:14:55.045 \nor when they email, have somebody call them as soon \n \n336 \n00:14:55.045 --> 00:14:56.445 \nas they come in and confirm \n \n337 \n00:14:56.585 --> 00:14:58.605 \nand ask them how many people are coming, \n \n338 \n00:14:58.995 --> 00:15:01.125 \nmake them feel like you want them there. \n \n339 \n00:15:01.675 --> 00:15:04.365 \nThis is our livelihood. We gotta take care of them. \n \n340 \n00:15:04.365 --> 00:15:08.045 \nThese are, these are like the, the brand new little babies \n \n\n341 \n00:15:08.235 --> 00:15:09.445 \nthat we have to nurture \n \n342 \n00:15:09.825 --> 00:15:12.285 \nand take them all the way through to the finish line. \n \n343 \n00:15:12.865 --> 00:15:15.205 \nAnd so, so many people are like, \n \n344 \n00:15:15.205 --> 00:15:16.285 \noh, I don't wanna deal with that. \n \n345 \n00:15:16.405 --> 00:15:18.005 \nI I want the automation to deal with it. \n \n346 \n00:15:18.035 --> 00:15:20.605 \nNothing beats personalization, nothing. \n \n347 \n00:15:21.225 --> 00:15:22.445 \nSo automation's great, \n \n348 \n00:15:22.865 --> 00:15:24.325 \nbut we're not trying to, we're not trying \n \n349 \n00:15:24.325 --> 00:15:25.885 \nto recruit a thousand people here. \n \n350 \n00:15:26.175 --> 00:15:28.645 \nWe've got 30, 40 people that we're dealing with. \n \n351 \n00:15:28.735 --> 00:15:30.005 \nLet's make sure they know \n \n\n352 \n00:15:30.005 --> 00:15:32.125 \nthat we care whether or not they show up. \n \n353 \n00:15:32.225 --> 00:15:33.285 \nSo make a phone call. \n \n354 \n00:15:33.595 --> 00:15:35.165 \nMake sure you do the follow up process \n \n355 \n00:15:35.345 --> 00:15:36.565 \nthe way that we've got it laid out. \n \n356 \n00:15:36.675 --> 00:15:39.925 \nCall 'em the day before, send a text message the day before. \n \n357 \n00:15:40.075 --> 00:15:42.725 \nMake sure you know who's coming you, if you, \n \n358 \n00:15:42.745 --> 00:15:45.605 \nif you don't do this properly, you could literally end up \n \n359 \n00:15:45.605 --> 00:15:46.805 \nwith too many people there. \n \n360 \n00:15:46.865 --> 00:15:48.005 \nAnd that's a problem, right? \n \n361 \n00:15:48.005 --> 00:15:49.165 \nIf you have to turn away 10 \n \n362 \n00:15:49.165 --> 00:15:50.845 \nor 20 people, it can be kind of awkward. \n \n\n363 \n00:15:51.225 --> 00:15:55.165 \nAnd so in some cases, we have enough people confirm that. \n \n364 \n00:15:55.165 --> 00:15:56.965 \nWe then tell the rest of the people who've \n \n365 \n00:15:57.205 --> 00:15:58.325 \nregistered do not come. \n \n366 \n00:15:58.585 --> 00:16:01.445 \nYou didn't confirm on time. You can come to the next one. \n \n367 \n00:16:01.625 --> 00:16:03.205 \nAnd that's good to know ahead of time so \n \n368 \n00:16:03.205 --> 00:16:05.565 \nthat you can manage the size of the room \n \n369 \n00:16:05.565 --> 00:16:06.805 \nand the number of people that show up. \n \n370 \n00:16:07.315 --> 00:16:10.885 \nWhat do you do if people do show up and you want, um, \n \n371 \n00:16:12.135 --> 00:16:13.515 \nand, and you don't have seats for 'em? \n \n372 \n00:16:13.515 --> 00:16:16.755 \nThere's two options. One, you can literally turn them away \n \n373 \n00:16:17.215 --> 00:16:18.915 \nand you can say, I'm sorry, we're full. \n \n\n374 \n00:16:19.095 --> 00:16:20.715 \nWe have another event coming up in two weeks. \n \n375 \n00:16:21.125 --> 00:16:22.875 \nWe'll have you come to that one. \n \n376 \n00:16:23.375 --> 00:16:25.235 \nLet me make sure that you're taken care of. \n \n377 \n00:16:25.455 --> 00:16:26.635 \nHere's a, here's a copy \n \n378 \n00:16:26.635 --> 00:16:29.155 \nof the movie you can watch on your own, et cetera. \n \n379 \n00:16:29.155 --> 00:16:32.275 \nYou can have streaming co cards that you hand out \n \n380 \n00:16:32.335 --> 00:16:33.355 \nto have your code on them \n \n381 \n00:16:33.535 --> 00:16:36.155 \nand you can at least give them something to mitigate that. \n \n382 \n00:16:36.415 --> 00:16:37.795 \nThe other option is bring in more chairs. \n \n383 \n00:16:38.085 --> 00:16:40.075 \nWe've had people to say, Hey, the dinners are gone, \n \n384 \n00:16:40.075 --> 00:16:42.395 \nbut you can still stay for the movie, da da da. \n \n\n385 \n00:16:42.495 --> 00:16:44.835 \nThat's another option, assuming that you're out of food. \n \n386 \n00:16:45.015 --> 00:16:46.235 \nThe other third option is just \n \n387 \n00:16:46.235 --> 00:16:47.595 \nto have the restaurant bring in more food \n \n388 \n00:16:47.615 --> 00:16:49.755 \nand, you know, we'll take care of it that, \n \n389 \n00:16:49.775 --> 00:16:52.875 \nbut okay, um, questions are, \n \n390 \n00:16:54.255 --> 00:16:56.915 \ndo we send people, you know, something in the mail? \n \n391 \n00:16:56.915 --> 00:16:58.915 \nYou certainly can send a letter in the mail. \n \n392 \n00:16:59.375 --> 00:17:03.115 \nUm, and do we tell them in the email follow up? \n \n393 \n00:17:03.415 --> 00:17:04.715 \nIs there gonna be food there? And I, \n \n394 \n00:17:04.835 --> 00:17:06.155 \nI I just don't understand this. \n \n395 \n00:17:07.155 --> 00:17:10.695 \nWe want people in our event, we've created an offer \n \n\n396 \n00:17:11.285 --> 00:17:15.135 \nthat gets people to show up and we wanna hide it somehow. \n \n397 \n00:17:15.755 --> 00:17:17.855 \nNo, that's not how this works. \n \n398 \n00:17:18.685 --> 00:17:21.895 \nIt's like any business that creates any kind of loss leader \n \n399 \n00:17:22.445 --> 00:17:24.815 \njust knows that they have a loss leader \n \n400 \n00:17:25.565 --> 00:17:27.865 \nand they're willing to use the loss leader \n \n401 \n00:17:27.865 --> 00:17:29.905 \nbecause they understand their economic support, \n \n402 \n00:17:30.275 --> 00:17:32.145 \neverything else that comes down the line. \n \n403 \n00:17:32.565 --> 00:17:34.665 \nSo whether that's the grocery store \n \n404 \n00:17:34.665 --> 00:17:37.305 \nthat puts everything on sale out on the end caps \n \n405 \n00:17:37.495 --> 00:17:39.665 \nthat are cheaper than what they're paying for it, \n \n406 \n00:17:40.615 --> 00:17:41.955 \nand then they know, well, \n \n\n407 \n00:17:41.955 --> 00:17:45.035 \nat least I got 'em in here on the cheap chips, \n \n408 \n00:17:45.415 --> 00:17:48.235 \nso now I can send that, sell them expensive. \n \n409 \n00:17:48.235 --> 00:17:51.525 \nEverything else. Like that's just business 1 0 1. \n \n410 \n00:17:51.785 --> 00:17:54.245 \nAnd that's what this is. Think about it as a loss leader, \n \n411 \n00:17:54.625 --> 00:17:56.525 \nyou know that you're gonna lose money on the deal, \n \n412 \n00:17:56.865 --> 00:18:00.645 \nbut it's the way that you have, it's the offer that, that \n \n413 \n00:18:01.525 --> 00:18:04.165 \ngenerates the activity that turns into \n \n414 \n00:18:04.235 --> 00:18:05.685 \nmassive amounts of income. \n \n415 \n00:18:05.775 --> 00:18:08.125 \nThere is no other business that I know of \n \n416 \n00:18:08.125 --> 00:18:11.645 \nthat may can make you more money faster than doing this, \n \n417 \n00:18:12.055 --> 00:18:13.805 \nespecially the way we've got it laid out. \n \n\n418 \n00:18:14.345 --> 00:18:16.805 \nUh, okay, technology, what do we do for tools, \n \n419 \n00:18:16.805 --> 00:18:18.005 \nsystems and tech setup? \n \n420 \n00:18:18.665 --> 00:18:23.525 \nUh, the recommendation is to find a venue that has a screen \n \n421 \n00:18:24.265 --> 00:18:27.205 \nand then you can bring a projector or use theirs. \n \n422 \n00:18:27.205 --> 00:18:28.645 \nSometimes they charge more for it. \n \n423 \n00:18:28.645 --> 00:18:29.725 \nSo you can just buy your own \n \n424 \n00:18:30.105 --> 00:18:33.685 \nand then plug in a laptop, stream the movie from the laptop \n \n425 \n00:18:34.105 --> 00:18:35.125 \nto your projector \n \n426 \n00:18:35.625 --> 00:18:38.685 \nand play it on their, play it on their screen. \n \n427 \n00:18:38.785 --> 00:18:42.645 \nThat's option eight is projector, laptop screen. \n \n428 \n00:18:43.025 --> 00:18:44.525 \nYou can bring your own screen too. \n \n\n429 \n00:18:44.725 --> 00:18:46.525 \nI mean, these are not expensive, right? \n \n430 \n00:18:46.785 --> 00:18:50.285 \nGo to Amazon, buy a screen for 150 bucks, get a projector \n \n431 \n00:18:50.585 --> 00:18:53.765 \nand play it in a way that you can control everything. \n \n432 \n00:18:54.145 --> 00:18:56.965 \nGet a Bluetooth speaker so that it's loud enough. \n \n433 \n00:18:57.025 --> 00:18:58.925 \nDo not play the audio off your laptop. \n \n434 \n00:18:59.515 --> 00:19:01.765 \nPlug in to your laptop with a speaker \n \n435 \n00:19:01.865 --> 00:19:03.245 \nor get a Bluetooth speaker. \n \n436 \n00:19:04.265 --> 00:19:08.885 \nDo not use your laptop speakers. Uh, so that's it. \n \n437 \n00:19:09.385 --> 00:19:12.485 \nYou can also use a big screen TV if they have one in the \n \n438 \n00:19:12.485 --> 00:19:15.445 \nroom and you can just plug directly into that with an HDMI. \n \n439 \n00:19:15.865 --> 00:19:17.525 \nAnd still you're gonna want speakers \n \n\n440 \n00:19:17.525 --> 00:19:20.325 \nthat plug into your laptops so that you're getting audio. \n \n441 \n00:19:20.875 --> 00:19:23.605 \nLike people can forgive bad video. \n \n442 \n00:19:23.715 --> 00:19:25.045 \nThey will not forgive bad audio. \n \n443 \n00:19:25.305 --> 00:19:27.605 \nIf they can't hear, they don't understand what's going on, \n \n444 \n00:19:27.715 --> 00:19:29.365 \nthey will not be happy. \n \n445 \n00:19:29.705 --> 00:19:31.525 \nAnd if they're not happy, they're not gonna convert. \n \n446 \n00:19:31.905 --> 00:19:35.325 \nSo make sure you get some sort of powered speaker \n \n447 \n00:19:35.465 --> 00:19:37.885 \nor Bluetooth speaker that is loud enough to fill the room. \n \n448 \n00:19:38.185 --> 00:19:41.125 \nAnd you probably want to test it before you get in there. \n \n449 \n00:19:41.385 --> 00:19:43.125 \nSo you can sit at the back of the room, \n \n450 \n00:19:43.235 --> 00:19:44.925 \nhave somebody clank a bunch of plates \n \n\n451 \n00:19:44.945 --> 00:19:46.605 \nand see if they can hear what's going on. \n \n452 \n00:19:47.145 --> 00:19:49.965 \nUh, that's it. This is not a complicated tech setup. \n \n453 \n00:19:50.395 --> 00:19:53.565 \nIt's stream the movie, use your own phone, plug it in \n \n454 \n00:19:53.565 --> 00:19:56.165 \nso your battery doesn't die with your using your hotspot. \n \n455 \n00:19:56.505 --> 00:19:58.365 \nAnd make sure that you can stream \n \n456 \n00:19:58.565 --> 00:20:00.005 \nthat movie without interruption. \n \n457 \n00:20:00.385 --> 00:20:03.485 \nUh, we will also give you a link to the pre-roll. \n \n458 \n00:20:03.905 --> 00:20:06.885 \nSo we'll talk about that now. What's the flow of this event? \n \n459 \n00:20:07.075 --> 00:20:09.285 \nOkay, so the flow is this. \n \n460 \n00:20:11.035 --> 00:20:13.285 \nHave people arrive 30 minutes before a movie. \n \n461 \n00:20:13.395 --> 00:20:14.565 \nTell them that's when dinner is. \n \n\n462 \n00:20:14.625 --> 00:20:16.005 \nSo the movie's gonna be a six 30. \n \n463 \n00:20:16.275 --> 00:20:19.205 \nTell them to get there at six. Dinner is served at six. \n \n464 \n00:20:19.635 --> 00:20:20.925 \nShow up early to get your dinner \n \n465 \n00:20:21.065 --> 00:20:24.165 \nand make sure that you're, you know, getting a good seat. \n \n466 \n00:20:24.785 --> 00:20:28.805 \nUh, then have a staff member at the door of the venue \n \n467 \n00:20:29.185 --> 00:20:30.485 \nto guide them to the room. \n \n468 \n00:20:31.155 --> 00:20:33.365 \nI've seen it so many times where we're in the back room \n \n469 \n00:20:33.945 --> 00:20:35.925 \nand people are wandering around wondering, \n \n470 \n00:20:35.925 --> 00:20:37.005 \nwhere is this place? \n \n471 \n00:20:37.005 --> 00:20:38.045 \nEven if you have a signup, \n \n472 \n00:20:38.045 --> 00:20:39.565 \nthey don't know exactly which way to go. \n \n\n473 \n00:20:39.785 --> 00:20:41.725 \nSo make sure you have somebody there at the front door \n \n474 \n00:20:41.745 --> 00:20:43.215 \nto guide them free. \n \n475 \n00:20:43.405 --> 00:20:45.975 \nHave your banners up, put something up that looks nice, \n \n476 \n00:20:46.165 --> 00:20:48.325 \nmake this a big presentation. \n \n477 \n00:20:48.395 --> 00:20:50.485 \nMake it a show. Make it professional. \n \n478 \n00:20:51.145 --> 00:20:53.765 \nUm, then have them come in \n \n479 \n00:20:53.785 --> 00:20:55.525 \nand they're gonna get their food in the \n \n480 \n00:20:55.525 --> 00:20:56.805 \nbuffet as they start. \n \n481 \n00:20:57.025 --> 00:20:59.165 \nNow I can already hear the objections. \n \n482 \n00:20:59.165 --> 00:21:01.125 \nWell, what if people come in and eat and leave? \n \n483 \n00:21:01.945 --> 00:21:04.645 \nWe do not have a problem with that happening regularly. \n \n\n484 \n00:21:04.915 --> 00:21:07.685 \nWill it happen to you? Most definitely it will. \n \n485 \n00:21:08.375 --> 00:21:10.225 \nDoes that matter? Most definitely. \n \n486 \n00:21:10.285 --> 00:21:12.925 \nIt does not, don't even worry about it. \n \n487 \n00:21:12.975 --> 00:21:14.605 \nDon't bat an eye, don't think about it. \n \n488 \n00:21:14.605 --> 00:21:15.925 \nDon't let it kill your mojo. \n \n489 \n00:21:16.305 --> 00:21:18.365 \nIt does not matter if somebody eats \n \n490 \n00:21:18.365 --> 00:21:20.325 \nand leaps, they're the idiots that showed up \n \n491 \n00:21:20.545 --> 00:21:23.085 \nand didn't even spend the requisite time \n \n492 \n00:21:23.345 --> 00:21:25.845 \nand commitment to save their own retirement. \n \n493 \n00:21:25.995 --> 00:21:28.405 \nOkay? So that's their problem. It's not my problem. \n \n494 \n00:21:28.805 --> 00:21:30.365 \nI don't care if somebody gets up and leaves. \n \n\n495 \n00:21:30.635 --> 00:21:32.125 \nIt's not gonna hurt me emotionally. \n \n496 \n00:21:32.265 --> 00:21:34.005 \nI'm not emotionally tied to whether \n \n497 \n00:21:34.005 --> 00:21:36.285 \nor not everybody sits there and watches my show and, \n \n498 \n00:21:36.305 --> 00:21:37.725 \nand schedules an appointment with me. \n \n499 \n00:21:38.025 --> 00:21:41.605 \nAll I care about is writing business. That's it. \n \n500 \n00:21:41.865 --> 00:21:42.885 \nIf I come out of there \n \n501 \n00:21:42.885 --> 00:21:46.125 \nand I can write 300, 500, 700,000 on every single event, \n \n502 \n00:21:46.395 --> 00:21:48.605 \nwhat do I care what the rest of these people did? \n \n503 \n00:21:48.965 --> 00:21:53.165 \nI don't. And so don't let that screw up the flow \n \n504 \n00:21:53.355 --> 00:21:54.765 \nthat it, that we know works. \n \n505 \n00:21:55.065 --> 00:21:56.645 \nAnd the flow is get 'em in. \n \n\n506 \n00:21:56.645 --> 00:21:58.285 \nWe've got the buffet, let them eat \n \n507 \n00:21:58.355 --> 00:22:00.045 \nbecause here's the cardinal scent. \n \n508 \n00:22:00.745 --> 00:22:03.485 \nYou do not want to be pitching and talking \n \n509 \n00:22:03.905 --> 00:22:06.965 \nand trying to get them to pay attention to your offer \n \n510 \n00:22:07.735 --> 00:22:09.925 \nwhile they are distracted with eating food \n \n511 \n00:22:10.065 --> 00:22:11.485 \nor fooling around with anything else. \n \n512 \n00:22:12.105 --> 00:22:13.765 \nYou need their undivided attention. \n \n513 \n00:22:14.345 --> 00:22:15.445 \nAnd so people that do this \n \n514 \n00:22:15.445 --> 00:22:17.965 \nand they're pitching while the food's being, you know, \n \n515 \n00:22:17.965 --> 00:22:20.405 \nbrought out and there's plates clanking \n \n516 \n00:22:20.665 --> 00:22:23.205 \nand the the movie's playing and they can't understand it \n \n\n517 \n00:22:23.205 --> 00:22:25.205 \nor hear it because there's so much going on. \n \n518 \n00:22:25.915 --> 00:22:29.045 \nWhat are you doing? You're literally shooting yourself in \n \n519 \n00:22:29.045 --> 00:22:30.925 \nyour own foot because you're \n \n520 \n00:22:31.725 --> 00:22:34.765 \ndistracting your audience from the message because why? \n \n521 \n00:22:34.765 --> 00:22:36.525 \nYou want to, you want to twist their arm \n \n522 \n00:22:36.715 --> 00:22:37.845 \ninto staying longer. \n \n523 \n00:22:38.625 --> 00:22:41.045 \nForget it. Like if, if they were the ones \n \n524 \n00:22:41.045 --> 00:22:42.325 \nthat were gonna dine and dash anyways, \n \n525 \n00:22:42.585 --> 00:22:43.725 \ndo you want them as a client? \n \n526 \n00:22:43.785 --> 00:22:45.205 \nYou want them as a, \n \n527 \n00:22:45.205 --> 00:22:46.645 \nas a potential appointment's gonna \n \n\n528 \n00:22:46.645 --> 00:22:47.765 \nwaste an hour of your time? \n \n529 \n00:22:48.165 --> 00:22:49.245 \nI don't think, I don't think so. \n \n530 \n00:22:49.485 --> 00:22:50.925 \nI think I'd rather have 'em dine and bash \n \n531 \n00:22:50.985 --> 00:22:52.285 \nand then I don't have to deal with that. \n \n532 \n00:22:52.745 --> 00:22:53.965 \nSo the flow is this. \n \n533 \n00:22:53.995 --> 00:22:55.765 \nThey come in, they get their food, \n \n534 \n00:22:56.035 --> 00:22:58.605 \nthey watch the preroll trailer package while they're eating. \n \n535 \n00:22:58.835 --> 00:23:00.845 \nThat gets them, you know, in fun. \n \n536 \n00:23:01.135 --> 00:23:02.845 \nLet's a, it's a seminar terminology \n \n537 \n00:23:02.845 --> 00:23:04.965 \nthat you get them in the right frame of mind. \n \n538 \n00:23:04.965 --> 00:23:08.125 \nThey're happy, they're fed, they're enjoying the content \n \n\n539 \n00:23:08.155 --> 00:23:09.965 \nthat we've created in the preroll trailer package. \n \n540 \n00:23:10.155 --> 00:23:13.205 \nThen five minutes before the movie starts, you have your \n \n541 \n00:23:13.715 --> 00:23:16.405 \nassistant introduce you as the host of tonight's event. \n \n542 \n00:23:16.595 --> 00:23:18.805 \nThey come on. You're gonna get the script here down below \n \n543 \n00:23:18.805 --> 00:23:21.445 \nthat I'll give to you for opening and closing. \n \n544 \n00:23:22.065 --> 00:23:23.965 \nYou give your opening little pitch. \n \n545 \n00:23:25.025 --> 00:23:27.125 \nYou make sure that this is done right. \n \n546 \n00:23:27.695 --> 00:23:31.205 \nThere is nothing that is more important at this point \n \n547 \n00:23:31.205 --> 00:23:35.245 \nforward than making sure your pitches are on point. \n \n548 \n00:23:37.375 --> 00:23:38.975 \nPractice them, memorize them. \n \n549 \n00:23:39.555 --> 00:23:40.855 \nDo them in front of the mirror. \n \n\n550 \n00:23:40.915 --> 00:23:43.695 \nDo them in front of a camera. Have somebody analyze it. \n \n551 \n00:23:43.765 --> 00:23:45.405 \nMake sure that you're giving it right. \n \n552 \n00:23:45.785 --> 00:23:48.405 \nYou don't want to be up there fumbling around with this. \n \n553 \n00:23:48.985 --> 00:23:50.285 \nYou have to be confident. \n \n554 \n00:23:51.255 --> 00:23:54.595 \nThe confident will, will exude from you. \n \n555 \n00:23:54.735 --> 00:23:58.795 \nOr the lack of confidence will, will equally \n \n556 \n00:23:59.635 --> 00:24:01.715 \ndemonstrate itself to the audience to where they're like, \n \n557 \n00:24:01.975 --> 00:24:03.475 \noh, I don't know if I want that guy \n \n558 \n00:24:03.535 --> 00:24:06.635 \nor gal managing my money if they can't even host a \n \n559 \n00:24:06.895 --> 00:24:08.315 \nlittle event, right? \n \n560 \n00:24:08.495 --> 00:24:11.085 \nSo anyway, be prepared with that. \n \n\n561 \n00:24:11.585 --> 00:24:16.555 \nUm, like it says there, get the food at the buffet, uh, \n \n562 \n00:24:17.335 --> 00:24:19.355 \nand then after the movie comes off, \n \n563 \n00:24:19.655 --> 00:24:20.915 \nyou get up there and do your clothes. \n \n564 \n00:24:21.495 --> 00:24:24.395 \nIt does not have to be a 20 minute presentation. \n \n565 \n00:24:24.455 --> 00:24:26.235 \nYou don't need to be showing slides \n \n566 \n00:24:26.735 --> 00:24:28.875 \nand trying to educate people about annuities. \n \n567 \n00:24:29.495 --> 00:24:32.675 \nYou want one thing and one thing only. At this point. \n \n568 \n00:24:33.245 --> 00:24:35.245 \nAppointments booked on the calendar. \n \n569 \n00:24:36.445 --> 00:24:38.835 \nAfter those appointments are booked, you can hang around, \n \n570 \n00:24:39.015 --> 00:24:41.235 \nyou can chat it up, you can have conversations, \n \n571 \n00:24:41.775 --> 00:24:44.915 \nbut you don't want to turn this into a q and a. \n \n\n572 \n00:24:45.765 --> 00:24:47.015 \nThis is not a q and a. \n \n573 \n00:24:47.565 --> 00:24:50.215 \nThis is a opportunity for you to take the emotion \n \n574 \n00:24:50.215 --> 00:24:53.015 \nthat's in the room of all the cool stuff they just saw \n \n575 \n00:24:53.395 --> 00:24:56.735 \nand convert that into action and commitment on the calendar. \n \n576 \n00:24:57.605 --> 00:24:59.785 \nAnd I can't tell you how many advisors are like, Hey, \n \n577 \n00:24:59.785 --> 00:25:01.945 \nlet's open this thing up into a q and a \n \n578 \n00:25:02.085 --> 00:25:03.865 \nand then people start asking questions. \n \n579 \n00:25:03.885 --> 00:25:06.305 \nIf we go on tangents and we're talking about annuities \n \n580 \n00:25:06.305 --> 00:25:09.505 \nand products and fees, and people stand up and they walk out \n \n581 \n00:25:09.505 --> 00:25:10.945 \nbecause they're like, oh, this was great. \n \n582 \n00:25:10.975 --> 00:25:15.765 \nI've really learned a lot. Adios. No. So we got the close. \n \n\n583 \n00:25:16.345 --> 00:25:18.405 \nAs soon as the close is finishing up, \n \n584 \n00:25:18.555 --> 00:25:21.445 \nyour staff is handing out the clipboards, then you \n \n585 \n00:25:21.505 --> 00:25:23.645 \nand your staff are watching everything going on. \n \n586 \n00:25:23.645 --> 00:25:26.325 \nAnd as soon as somebody looks like they're about finished \n \n587 \n00:25:26.325 --> 00:25:28.805 \nwith their clipboard, you're it, you're right there \n \n588 \n00:25:28.805 --> 00:25:31.605 \nwith them with your calendar saying, great, thank you. \n \n589 \n00:25:32.055 --> 00:25:35.725 \nLet's get it on the calendar for a time to meet one \n \n590 \n00:25:35.725 --> 00:25:36.965 \nby one by one by one. \n \n591 \n00:25:37.305 --> 00:25:38.525 \nDo not ask them to go to the back \n \n592 \n00:25:38.525 --> 00:25:39.885 \nof the room and book an appointment. \n \n593 \n00:25:40.225 --> 00:25:41.365 \nDo not ask them to get up \n \n\n594 \n00:25:41.465 --> 00:25:43.605 \nand drop off their clipboards and we'll call you back. \n \n595 \n00:25:43.985 --> 00:25:46.405 \nDo not ask them to do anything other than sit there \n \n596 \n00:25:46.745 --> 00:25:48.445 \nand schedule an appointment with you \n \n597 \n00:25:48.505 --> 00:25:50.605 \nor your staff while they're sitting \n \n598 \n00:25:50.605 --> 00:25:51.965 \ndown together at the table. \n \n599 \n00:25:54.235 --> 00:25:57.945 \nAll of this is to generate maximum appointments. \n \n600 \n00:25:58.405 --> 00:26:01.425 \nYou should be booking half the room if you aren't booking \n \n601 \n00:26:01.425 --> 00:26:03.425 \nhalf the room in terms of actual buying \n \n602 \n00:26:03.425 --> 00:26:04.585 \nunits to appointments. \n \n603 \n00:26:04.925 --> 00:26:07.105 \nSo if you've got 30 people there, 22 households, \n \n604 \n00:26:07.105 --> 00:26:08.545 \nyou need 11 appointments outta that. \n \n\n605 \n00:26:09.045 --> 00:26:11.585 \nIf you're not getting 11 appointments, record yourself. \n \n606 \n00:26:12.055 --> 00:26:13.905 \nSend it in, we'll help you so \n \n607 \n00:26:13.905 --> 00:26:14.985 \nthat you can improve your clothes. \n \n608 \n00:26:16.035 --> 00:26:17.885 \nAlso, take pictures of your events. \n \n609 \n00:26:18.305 --> 00:26:21.005 \nWe can use those in other marketing. Send 'em into us. \n \n610 \n00:26:21.385 --> 00:26:23.165 \nWe, we want to celebrate that with you \n \n611 \n00:26:23.165 --> 00:26:26.125 \nbecause it's an awesome, uh, experience to do these. \n \n612 \n00:26:26.665 --> 00:26:28.325 \nUh, okay, so that's the flow. \n \n613 \n00:26:29.145 --> 00:26:31.355 \nCome in, eat your food, watch the pre-roll, \n \n614 \n00:26:31.575 --> 00:26:34.635 \nyou do your intro, play the movie outro, set appointments. \n \n615 \n00:26:34.765 --> 00:26:36.555 \nAfter that, you can be buddy \n \n\n616 \n00:26:36.755 --> 00:26:37.955 \nbuddies with everybody you want. \n \n617 \n00:26:38.725 --> 00:26:41.615 \nOkay? Uh, so that's it. \n \n618 \n00:26:41.855 --> 00:26:43.925 \nI mean, opening and closing, we talked about that. \n \n619 \n00:26:44.185 --> 00:26:45.645 \nHow to book the appointments, et cetera. \n \n620 \n00:26:46.825 --> 00:26:49.525 \nThe scripts to the open and the close are down below. \n \n621 \n00:26:50.165 --> 00:26:53.285 \nI would highly recommend that you use what we are providing \n \n622 \n00:26:53.285 --> 00:26:54.645 \nto you verbatim. \n \n623 \n00:26:55.545 --> 00:26:58.805 \nThe reason is because we've done hundreds and hundreds \n \n624 \n00:26:58.805 --> 00:27:00.645 \nand hundreds of these and we know what works. \n \n625 \n00:27:01.565 --> 00:27:03.185 \nAnd I know most advisors are \n \n626 \n00:27:03.185 --> 00:27:04.305 \ngonna think, oh, well you know what? \n \n\n627 \n00:27:04.325 --> 00:27:05.505 \nI'm gonna ad-lib this. I'm \n \n628 \n00:27:05.505 --> 00:27:06.585 \ngonna add this, I'm gonna do this. \n \n629 \n00:27:06.605 --> 00:27:10.385 \nOr the please until you're writing a million a month. \n \n630 \n00:27:10.995 --> 00:27:12.465 \nWrite, just follow our system. \n \n631 \n00:27:13.175 --> 00:27:16.905 \nThen, like I said, the proven track record trumps all. \n \n632 \n00:27:17.245 --> 00:27:18.425 \nSo as soon as you're doing that \n \n633 \n00:27:18.485 --> 00:27:20.625 \nand you feel like you wanna fool around with something else, \n \n634 \n00:27:21.015 --> 00:27:23.165 \nthen I'll be my guest. \n \n635 \n00:27:24.145 --> 00:27:27.885 \nBut there is, there, there's psychology built into this. \n \n636 \n00:27:28.535 --> 00:27:30.805 \nThere is proof built into this. \n \n637 \n00:27:30.895 --> 00:27:35.595 \nThere is a track record built into this intro, outro, \n \n\n638 \n00:27:35.775 --> 00:27:38.155 \nand process that we know works if you'll use it. \n \n639 \n00:27:38.295 --> 00:27:41.315 \nSo that's it for this fast start. \n \n640 \n00:27:41.455 --> 00:27:43.555 \nThat's everything you need to know, uh, \n \n641 \n00:27:43.655 --> 00:27:47.195 \nat a broad strokes overview to be a successful, uh, \n \n642 \n00:27:47.195 --> 00:27:48.635 \nto put on a successful event. \n \n643 \n00:27:48.905 --> 00:27:51.275 \nMake sure you download the scripts below, go through those. \n \n644 \n00:27:51.625 --> 00:27:53.035 \nI'll do another little training for that \n \n645 \n00:27:53.175 --> 00:27:54.595 \nand just walk you through those scripts. \n \n646 \n00:27:54.595 --> 00:27:56.955 \nBut for now, that's it. Hey, let's get going. \n \n ",
